{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LtGF8r1NFo9syNbubCmCGp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: worst\n2\tnarrative_guidance: 'Reframe away from ''HS beats text'' toward one of two angles,\n3\t  both still targeting EMNLP Main: (A) **Negative-results-first framing**: ''We systematically\n4\t  evaluate 7 detection methods across 4 reasoning models and find that none achieves\n5\t  deployment-ready performance. The best approach (temporal HS probes) still has 39-62%\n6\t  safe FPR. We identify layer selection bias as a critical confounder and provide\n7\t  a sensitivity analysis framework.'' This positions the paper as a cautionary benchmark.\n8\t  (B) **Methodology-first framing**: ''We discover that a commonly used layer selection\n9\t  metric systematically biases hidden-state evaluations, producing false negatives\n10\t  at scale. We demonstrate this across 4 models and 5 metrics, show it reverses published\n11\t  conclusions, and propose a multi-metric audit protocol.'' This makes layer selection\n12\t  the headline and HS-vs-text becomes supporting evidence. Either angle requires:\n13\t  toning down deployment language, foregrounding limitations honestly, positioning\n14\t  as ''the field needs this evaluation before building on shaky foundations.'' Add\n15\t  cost-benefit analysis (HS access cost vs marginal gain over text) to preempt the\n16\t  engineering-cost reviewer.\n17\t\n18\t  '\n19\timagined_experiments:\n20\t- name: Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b & QwQ-32B)\n21\t  plan_id: exp-001\n22\t  description: 'Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract\n23\t    hidden states at all layers, evaluate static LR probes per layer under multiple\n24\t    selection metrics. Includes discovery of layer selection bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate\n25\t    → shallow layers).\n26\t\n27\t    '\n28\t  expected_results:\n29\t    r1_32b_biased_layer: 10\n30\t    r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct: 15.6\n31\t    r1_32b_corrected_layer: 63\n32\t    r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 98.4\n33\t    r1_32b_l10_bal_acc: 0.718\n34\t    r1_32b_l63_bal_acc_w1: 0.826\n35\t    r1_32b_n_test: 20\n36\t    r1_32b_l63_precision_w1: 0.603\n37\t    r1_32b_l63_fpr_w1: 0.235\n38\t    qwq_32b_biased_layer: 2\n39\t    qwq_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct: 3.1\n40\t    qwq_32b_corrected_layer: 60\n41\t    qwq_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 93.8\n42\t    qwq_32b_l2_bal_acc: 0.73\n43\t    qwq_32b_l60_bal_acc_w3: 0.74\n44\t    qwq_32b_n_test: 19-20\n45\t    qwq_32b_l60_precision_w3: 0.578\n46\t    qwq_32b_l60_fpr_w3: 0.279\n47\t    layer_selection_bias_discovered: true\n48\t    metrics_tested: 5\n49\t    biased_metric: bal_acc × crossing_rate\n50\t    loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct: 65-68%\n51\t  result_rationale: 'Layer sweep reveals a genuine methodological finding (biased\n52\t    metric selects shallow layers), and corrected layers do show HS advantage. However,\n53\t    worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \"Finding that one composite metric is bad\n54\t    is trivially expected — practitioners routinely try multiple metrics. This is\n55\t    hyperparameter tuning, not a contribution.\" (2) n=19-20 test traces for 32B models\n56\t    mean bootstrap CIs are wide. MDE analysis shows R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp)\n57\t    is far below the 11.2pp MDE at 80% power — the NS result for position-mediated\n58\t    HS advantage is a power limitation, not evidence. Reviewers note: \"you can''t\n59\t    claim the advantage is ''real but position-mediated'' when you''re underpowered\n60\t    to detect the non-positional component.\" (3) The corrected layers (L60/L63, >93%\n61\t    depth) are so deep they''re essentially the output layer — reviewer asks: \"are\n62\t    you just detecting output-token semantics via a different pathway?\"\n63\t\n64\t    '\n65\t  tables:\n66\t  - name: Layer Selection Metric × Model → Selected Layer\n67\t    columns:\n68\t    - Metric\n69\t    - R1-8B\n70\t    - OT-7B\n71\t    - QwQ-32B\n72\t    - R1-32B\n73\t    rows:\n74\t    - - bal_acc × crossing_rate\n75\t      - L0 (0%)\n76\t      - L0 (0%)\n77\t      - L2 (3%)\n78\t      - L10 (16%)\n79\t    - - pure bal_acc\n80\t      - L12 (39%)\n81\t      - L9 (32%)\n82\t      - L30 (47%)\n83\t      - L47 (73%)\n84\t    - - precision\n85\t      - L16 (52%)\n86\t      - L16 (57%)\n87\t      - L60 (94%)\n88\t      - L63 (98%)\n89\t    - - pre_cp_fpr\n90\t      - L16 (52%)\n91\t      - L16 (57%)\n92\t      - L60 (94%)\n93\t      - L63 (98%)\n94\t    - - composite (additive)\n95\t      - L16 (52%)\n96\t      - L12 (43%)\n97\t      - L60 (94%)\n98\t      - L63 (98%)\n99\t  figures_description:\n100\t  - 'Heatmap: 5 metrics × 4 models → selected layer depth (%). Clear shallow-bias\n101\t    pattern for crossing_rate metric on 32B. Threshold-dependent metrics converge\n102\t    on deep layers.'\n103\t  analysis: 'Layer selection sensitivity is the paper''s primary methodological contribution,\n104\t    but worst-tier reviewers rate it insufficient novelty (2.0-2.5). The argument:\n105\t    \"every ML practitioner knows different metrics select different hyperparameters.\n106\t    Showing this for layer selection is an observation, not a method. There''s no\n107\t    proposed algorithm to automatically resolve the disagreement — just ''try more\n108\t    metrics,'' which is standard practice.\"\n109\t\n110\t    '\n111\t- name: Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\n112\t  plan_id: exp-002\n113\t  description: 'Temporal LR at multiple window sizes on R1-32B (L63) and QwQ-32B (L60).\n114\t    Evaluate whether temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across 32B\n115\t    models.\n116\t\n117\t    '\n118\t  expected_results:\n119\t    r1_32b_l63_w1_bal_acc: 0.826\n120\t    r1_32b_l63_w1_precision: 0.603\n121\t    r1_32b_l63_w1_coverage: 0.25\n122\t    r1_32b_l63_w3_bal_acc: 0.754\n123\t    r1_32b_l63_w5_bal_acc: 0.741\n124\t    r1_32b_l63_w10_bal_acc: 0.737\n125\t    r1_32b_l63_w15_bal_acc: 0.721\n126\t    r1_32b_temporal_trend: DECREASING with W (W=1 best, -10.5pp by W=15)\n127\t    r1_32b_dimensionality_issue: 51200 × W features for n=20 → massive overfitting\n128\t      at W≥3\n129\t    qwq_32b_l60_w1_bal_acc: 0.74\n130\t    qwq_32b_l60_w3_bal_acc: 0.74\n131\t    qwq_32b_l60_w5_bal_acc: 0.742\n132\t    qwq_32b_l60_w15_bal_acc: 0.731\n133\t    qwq_32b_temporal_trend: flat to slightly decreasing\n134\t    qwq_32b_best_w: 5\n135\t    text_temporal_universal_improvement: true\n136\t    hs_temporal_improvement_3_of_4: true\n137\t    hs_temporal_exception: OT-7B HS peaks W=3, declines at larger W; R1-32B only W=1\n138\t      significant\n139\t  result_rationale: 'Temporal context shows a split pattern at 32B: QwQ benefits modestly\n140\t    across all W (text and HS), but R1-32B DEGRADES with increasing W because concatenating\n141\t    51200-dimensional vectors across W>1 windows creates a feature space massively\n142\t    exceeding n=20 training samples. This is not a failure of the temporal hypothesis\n143\t    — it''s a sample-size bottleneck. Worst-tier reviewers seize on this: \"R1-32B\n144\t    temporal results are meaningless because you''re fitting 102400+ parameters to\n145\t    20 samples. The W=1 ''significance'' at L63 survives only because W=1 avoids this\n146\t    problem. Your ''best'' result for R1-32B is actually a dimensionality artifact\n147\t    — the only window that avoids overfitting happens to be significant.\" Claim 1\n148\t    (temporal universally helps) holds for text probes across all 4 models, but for\n149\t    HS probes, 2/4 models have caveats (OT-7B saturation, R1-32B dimensionality).\n150\t    Reviewers frame this as \"temporal improvement is a text-probe finding, not an\n151\t    HS-probe finding.\"\n152\t\n153\t    '\n154\t  tables:\n155\t  - name: Temporal Window Effect on 32B Models (HS probes, corrected layers)\n156\t    columns:\n157\t    - Model\n158\t    - Layer\n159\t    - W=1\n160\t    - W=3\n161\t    - W=5\n162\t    - W=10\n163\t    - W=15\n164\t    - Trend\n165\t    rows:\n166\t    - - R1-32B\n167\t      - L63\n168\t      - 0.826*\n169\t      - '0.754'\n170\t      - '0.741'\n171\t      - '0.737'\n172\t      - '0.721'\n173\t      - ↓ declining\n174\t    - - QwQ-32B\n175\t      - L60\n176\t      - '0.740'\n177\t      - '0.740'\n178\t      - '0.742'\n179\t      - '0.735'\n180\t      - '0.731'\n181\t      - → flat\n182\t    - - R1-8B\n183\t      - L14\n184\t      - '0.778'\n185\t      - '0.790'\n186\t      - '0.781'\n187\t      - '0.793'\n188\t      - '0.802'\n189\t      - ↑ improving\n190\t    - - OT-7B\n191\t      - L16\n192\t      - '0.663'\n193\t      - 0.688*\n194\t      - '0.678'\n195\t      - '0.668'\n196\t      - '0.659'\n197\t      - ↑↓ peaks W=3\n198\t  figures_description:\n199\t  - 'Line plot: bal_acc vs W for each model. R1-8B shows steady climb. OT-7B peaks\n200\t    then falls. QwQ flat. R1-32B declines from W=1. Divergent patterns undermine ''universal\n201\t    temporal benefit'' claim.'\n202\t  analysis: 'Worst-tier reviewers note: \"Temporal context helps universally for text\n203\t    probes (a known result — more context helps NLP classifiers). For HS probes, 2/4\n204\t    models show non-monotonic or declining trends. The paper''s Claim 1 should be\n205\t    ''temporal context helps text probes'' — attributing it to HS probes is cherry-picking\n206\t    the models where it works.\"\n207\t\n208\t    '\n209\t- name: Cross-model transfer probes\n210\t  plan_id: exp-003\n211\t  description: 'Train probe on one model, test on another. PCA projection for cross-architecture\n212\t    transfer (4096→5120 or vice versa). Tests whether safety commitment encoding is\n213\t    universal.\n214\t\n215\t    '\n216\t  expected_results:\n217\t    r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.522\n218\t    r1_8b_to_qwq_acc: 0.595\n219\t    r1_32b_to_qwq_acc: 0.673\n220\t    qwq_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.673\n221\t    r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.583\n222\t    best_transfer_overall: 0.673\n223\t    chance_baseline: 0.5\n224\t    all_transfers_below_within_model: true\n225\t    within_family_32b_weak_transfer: 0.673\n226\t    cross_family_at_chance: true\n227\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-model transfer fails completely — the best transfer accuracy\n228\t    (67.3% Qwen-family 32B internal) is far below within-model performance and barely\n229\t    above the majority-class baseline. Cross-family transfer (Llama↔Qwen) is at chance.\n230\t    Worst-tier reviewers use this as evidence against the paper: \"Safety encoding\n231\t    is model-specific. This means every new model needs its own probe training with\n232\t    labeled commitment points — a supervision cost the paper downplays. If safety\n233\t    commitment were a universal phenomenon, SOME geometric structure should transfer.\n234\t    The complete failure suggests the probes are learning model-specific artifacts,\n235\t    not a generalizable safety signal.\" This finding is consistent with the selectivity\n236\t    control task (7.5-18.5pp) but reviewers argue selectivity merely shows the probes\n237\t    aren''t random — not that they detect genuine safety commitment vs idiosyncratic\n238\t    model behavior.\n239\t\n240\t    '\n241\t  tables:\n242\t  - name: Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\n243\t    columns:\n244\t    - Train →\n245\t    - R1-8B\n246\t    - OT-7B\n247\t    - R1-32B\n248\t    - QwQ-32B\n249\t    rows:\n250\t    - - R1-8B\n251\t      - 0.817 (self)\n252\t      - —\n253\t      - '0.522'\n254\t      - '0.595'\n255\t    - - OT-7B\n256\t      - —\n257\t      - 0.688 (self)\n258\t      - —\n259\t      - —\n260\t    - - R1-32B\n261\t      - '0.583'\n262\t      - —\n263\t      - 0.826 (self)\n264\t      - '0.673'\n265\t    - - QwQ-32B\n266\t      - —\n267\t      - —\n268\t      - '0.673'\n269\t      - 0.740 (self)\n270\t  figures_description:\n271\t  - 'Heatmap: transfer accuracy matrix. Diagonal (self) shows 69-83%. Off-diagonal\n272\t    all below 68%. Cross-family cells (Llama↔Qwen) near chance (50-60%).'\n273\t  analysis: 'Complete transfer failure. Worst-tier reviewers conclude: \"The paper\n274\t    claims to study ''safety commitment detection'' as a general phenomenon, but the\n275\t    probes are entirely model-specific. This means the method has zero practical scalability\n276\t    — you need labeled data for every model you want to monitor. Combined with the\n277\t    engineering cost of white-box access, the deployment story collapses.\"\n278\t\n279\t    '\n280\t- name: Lead time analysis across models\n281\t  plan_id: exp-004\n282\t  description: 'Evaluate HS probe lead time distributions on all 4 models. Compare\n283\t    HS vs text lead times. Lead time = steps between first detection and commitment\n284\t    point.\n285\t\n286\t    '\n287\t  expected_results:\n288\t    r1_8b_hs_lead_mean: -0.1\n289\t    r1_8b_hs_lead_note: HS detects AT commitment, not before\n290\t    r1_8b_text_lead_mean: 10.3\n291\t    r1_8b_text_lead_inflated: high text lead is artifact of high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy)\n292\t    ot_7b_hs_lead_mean: 21.6\n293\t    ot_7b_text_lead_mean: 45.5\n294\t    ot_7b_lead_note: absolute values inflated by long traces (~80 sentences)\n295\t    qwq_32b_hs_lead_mean_range: -1.2 to +1.9\n296\t    qwq_32b_text_lead_mean_range: 3.7 to 12.3\n297\t    qwq_32b_hs_lead_negative: true\n298\t    qwq_32b_position_baseline_lead: better than HS\n299\t    r1_32b_hs_lead_mean: +11.7 to +13.3\n300\t    r1_32b_hs_lead_positive: true\n301\t    r1_32b_coverage_at_significant_w: 0.25\n302\t    r1_32b_lead_caveat: only 5/20 traces detected at W=1\n303\t    gru_r1_8b_lead: +4.5 ± 3.0\n304\t    gru_r1_8b_worst_seed: '+0.9'\n305\t  result_rationale: 'Lead time results are a mixed bag that worst-tier reviewers weaponize:\n306\t    (1) R1-8B HS lead is effectively zero — HS detects AT commitment, not before.\n307\t    Only GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9). Text lead of +10.3\n308\t    is misleadingly high (artifact of trigger-happy detection with 20.5% pre-CP FPR).\n309\t    (2) QwQ-32B: HS lead time is NEGATIVE (-1.2 to +1.9 steps) while text detects\n310\t    3.7-12.3 steps earlier. Position baseline (bal_acc=0.746) beats HS (bal_acc=0.740).\n311\t    A reviewer writes: \"for QwQ, just tracking relative position in the trace outperforms\n312\t    hidden-state monitoring — why bother with white-box access?\" (3) R1-32B has excellent\n313\t    HS lead (+13 steps) but only 25% coverage — meaning 15/20 traces are undetected.\n314\t    Reviewer: \"a detector that works on 5 traces is an anecdote, not a system.\" (4)\n315\t    OT-7B lead times are inflated by long traces. Overall: 2/4 models have HS lead\n316\t    ≤ 0, 1/4 has only 25% coverage, and 1/4 has inflated absolute values. No model\n317\t    presents a clean HS lead time story.\n318\t\n319\t    '\n320\t  tables:\n321\t  - name: 'Lead Time Comparison: HS vs Text vs Position'\n322\t    columns:\n323\t    - Model\n324\t    - HS Lead (steps)\n325\t    - Text Lead (steps)\n326\t    - Position BA\n327\t    - HS BA\n328\t    - HS > Position?\n329\t    rows:\n330\t    - - R1-8B (W=15)\n331\t      - −0.1\n332\t      - +10.3†\n333\t      - —\n334\t      - '0.802'\n335\t      - —\n336\t    - - OT-7B (W=3)\n337\t      - +21.6‡\n338\t      - +45.5‡\n339\t      - —\n340\t      - '0.688'\n341\t      - —\n342\t    - - QwQ-32B (W=3)\n343\t      - −1.2 to +1.9\n344\t      - +3.7 to +12.3\n345\t      - '0.746'\n346\t      - '0.740'\n347\t      - 'No'\n348\t    - - R1-32B (W=1)\n349\t      - +11.7 to +13.3\n350\t      - —\n351\t      - '0.686'\n352\t      - '0.826'\n353\t      - Yes (25% cov)\n354\t  figures_description:\n355\t  - Per-model lead time distribution boxplot. QwQ and R1-8B HS centered near zero.\n356\t    R1-32B shows positive lead but with 75% of traces undetected (marked as missing).\n357\t    Text leads generally positive but with high FPR caveats.\n358\t  analysis: 'Worst-tier reviewers identify lead time as the practical dealbreaker:\n359\t    \"The paper claims HS monitoring enables early detection, but 2/4 models show HS\n360\t    detecting LATER than text. The one model with good lead time (R1-32B) detects\n361\t    only 25% of traces. A deployment team would choose text monitoring over HS monitoring\n362\t    for lead time alone, saving the engineering cost of hidden-state extraction.\"\n363\t\n364\t    '\n365\t- name: TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (all 4 models)\n366\t  plan_id: exp-005\n367\t  description: 'Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace covariance across all 4 models.\n368\t    Tests whether geometric distance-based methods (TrajGuard-style) can detect safety\n369\t    commitment.\n370\t\n371\t    '\n372\t  expected_results:\n373\t    r1_8b_separation: 0.07\n374\t    ot_7b_separation: 0.03\n375\t    r1_32b_separation: 0.15\n376\t    qwq_32b_separation: 0.2\n377\t    max_separation_any_model: 0.2\n378\t    all_models_fail: true\n379\t    false_alarm_exceeds_tp_all_thresholds: true\n380\t    signal_location: minor PCA components, not principal directions\n381\t    r1_8b_safe_md: 6.66\n382\t    r1_8b_post_md: 6.73\n383\t    r1_32b_safe_md: 6.73\n384\t    r1_32b_post_md: 6.88\n385\t    qwq_32b_safe_md: 3.22\n386\t    qwq_32b_post_md: 3.42\n387\t  result_rationale: 'Mahalanobis distance fails on all 4 models (max separation 0.20\n388\t    on QwQ, FA rate ≥ TP rate at every threshold). The signal lives in minor PCA components,\n389\t    not principal variance directions — Mahalanobis, which weights by inverse covariance,\n390\t    is structurally blind to this geometry. Worst-tier reviewers have mixed reactions:\n391\t    (1) \"Mahalanobis failing is expected — Bolukbasi et al. 2016 and subsequent work\n392\t    showed safety-relevant features are often orthogonal to principal variance. This\n393\t    negative result is predictable, not informative.\" (2) \"You tested one geometric\n394\t    baseline. What about k-NN distance, local outlier factor, or isolation forests?\n395\t    The negative result space is underexplored.\" The finding supports the \"existing\n396\t    methods fail\" claim but reviewers rate it as incremental — failure of one known-limited\n397\t    baseline is not a contribution.\n398\t\n399\t    '\n400\t  tables:\n401\t  - name: Mahalanobis Distance Separation (4 models)\n402\t    columns:\n403\t    - Model\n404\t    - Layer\n405\t    - Safe MD\n406\t    - Post-CP MD\n407\t    - Separation\n408\t    - FA ≥ TP?\n409\t    rows:\n410\t    - - R1-8B\n411\t      - L14\n412\t      - '6.66'\n413\t      - '6.73'\n414\t      - '+0.07'\n415\t      - 'Yes'\n416\t    - - OT-7B\n417\t      - L16\n418\t      - '7.68'\n419\t      - '7.71'\n420\t      - '+0.03'\n421\t      - 'Yes'\n422\t    - - R1-32B\n423\t      - L63\n424\t      - '6.73'\n425\t      - '6.88'\n426\t      - '+0.15'\n427\t      - 'Yes'\n428\t    - - QwQ-32B\n429\t      - L60\n430\t      - '3.22'\n431\t      - '3.42'\n432\t      - '+0.20'\n433\t      - 'Yes'\n434\t  figures_description:\n435\t  - 'MD time-series: safe (blue) and jailbreak (red) traces completely overlapping\n436\t    on all 4 models. No visible discontinuity at commitment point.'\n437\t  analysis: 'Universal Mahalanobis failure confirms safety signal is geometrically\n438\t    inaccessible to distance-based methods. But worst-tier reviewers dismiss this:\n439\t    \"Showing Mahalanobis doesn''t work is a paragraph in a methods section, not a\n440\t    contribution. The field already knows unsupervised geometric methods struggle\n441\t    with fine-grained behavioral distinctions.\"\n442\t\n443\t    '\n444\t- name: Bootstrap confidence intervals + threshold-independent metrics\n445\t  plan_id: exp-006\n446\t  description: '10000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across 4 models. Holm-Bonferroni\n447\t    correction. AUROC, precision-recall curves, detection delay CDF.\n448\t\n449\t    '\n450\t  expected_results:\n451\t    holm_bonferroni_pass: 12/12\n452\t    r1_8b_precision_delta: +22.4pp\n453\t    r1_8b_precision_p: <0.001\n454\t    ot_7b_precision_delta: +21.9pp\n455\t    ot_7b_precision_p: <0.001\n456\t    qwq_32b_precision_delta: +23.6pp\n457\t    qwq_32b_precision_p: <0.001\n458\t    qwq_32b_fpr_p: 0.004\n459\t    r1_32b_precision_delta: +15.4pp\n460\t    r1_32b_precision_p: <0.001\n461\t    r1_32b_fpr_p: 0.007\n462\t    r1_32b_fpr_marginal_note: p=0.007 passes HB threshold 0.025 but barely\n463\t    marginal_tests_count: 2\n464\t    all_precision_deltas_positive: true\n465\t    precision_range: +15.4 to +23.6pp\n466\t    r1_32b_ci_width_ba: ~0.15\n467\t    qwq_32b_ci_width_ba: ~0.12\n468\t    r1_8b_ci_width_ba: ~0.06\n469\t  result_rationale: 'All 12/12 tests pass Holm-Bonferroni, which is statistically\n470\t    clean. But worst-tier reviewers attack the PRACTICAL significance, not the statistical\n471\t    significance: (1) \"Of course 12/12 pass — you selected the best layer AND the\n472\t    best window per model, then tested. The researcher degrees of freedom (4 models\n473\t    × 64 layers × 7 windows × 5 metrics) dwarf the 12 tests corrected for.\" (2) \"The\n474\t    CIs for 32B models are extremely wide (±0.15 for R1-32B bal_acc). Statistical\n475\t    significance with n=20 means the effect is large, but the PRECISION of the estimate\n476\t    is poor. You know HS is better somewhere between +3pp and +34pp — that''s not\n477\t    actionable.\" (3) \"R1-32B FPR p=0.007 and QwQ FPR p=0.004 are the most marginal\n478\t    passing tests. A 10001st bootstrap replicate could flip either.\" (4) \"Holm-Bonferroni\n479\t    corrects for 12 tests, but the true multiplicity (including layer/window selection)\n480\t    is orders of magnitude larger.\"\n481\t\n482\t    '\n483\t  tables:\n484\t  - name: Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12, α=0.05)\n485\t    columns:\n486\t    - Rank\n487\t    - Model\n488\t    - Metric\n489\t    - p-value\n490\t    - HB Threshold\n491\t    - Pass\n492\t    rows:\n493\t    - - 1\n494\t      - R1-8B\n495\t      - precision\n496\t      - <0.001\n497\t      - '0.0042'\n498\t      - ✅\n499\t    - - 2\n500\t      - OT-7B\n501\t      - bal_acc\n502\t      - <0.001\n503\t      - '0.0045'\n504\t      - ✅\n505\t    - - 3\n506\t      - OT-7B\n507\t      - precision\n508\t      - <0.001\n509\t      - '0.0050'\n510\t      - ✅\n511\t    - - 4\n512\t      - OT-7B\n513\t      - FPR\n514\t      - <0.001\n515\t      - '0.0056'\n516\t      - ✅\n517\t    - - 5\n518\t      - QwQ\n519\t      - bal_acc\n520\t      - <0.001\n521\t      - '0.0063'\n522\t      - ✅\n523\t    - - 6\n524\t      - QwQ\n525\t      - precision\n526\t      - <0.001\n527\t      - '0.0071'\n528\t      - ✅\n529\t    - - 7\n530\t      - R1-32B\n531\t      - bal_acc\n532\t      - <0.001\n533\t      - '0.0083'\n534\t      - ✅\n535\t    - - 8\n536\t      - R1-32B\n537\t      - precision\n538\t      - <0.001\n539\t      - '0.0100'\n540\t      - ✅\n541\t    - - 9\n542\t      - QwQ\n543\t      - FPR\n544\t      - '0.004'\n545\t      - '0.0125'\n546\t      - ✅\n547\t    - - 10\n548\t      - R1-8B\n549\t      - bal_acc\n550\t      - '0.005'\n551\t      - '0.0167'\n552\t      - ✅\n553\t    - - 11\n554\t      - R1-32B\n555\t      - FPR\n556\t      - '0.007'\n557\t      - '0.0250'\n558\t      - ✅\n559\t    - - 12\n560\t      - R1-8B\n561\t      - FPR\n562\t      - '0.044'\n563\t      - '0.0500'\n564\t      - ✅\n565\t  figures_description:\n566\t  - 'Forest plot: HS-text precision delta with 95% CI per model. All positive. R1-32B\n567\t    CI is widest (+3pp to +28pp), reflecting n=20 limitation.'\n568\t  - 'Bootstrap distribution of HS-text AUROC difference: 7-8B models clearly positive,\n569\t    32B models positive but with wider spread.'\n570\t  analysis: 'Statistical significance is established but reviewers find it insufficient:\n571\t    \"The paper''s statistical methodology is sound, but the story it tells is ''HS\n572\t    is significantly better by 15-24pp precision at best-case layer/window selection.''\n573\t    For an EMNLP Main paper, we need either (a) a novel method that achieves these\n574\t    gains, or (b) a surprising finding. Neither: linear probes on hidden states are\n575\t    standard, and it''s unsurprising they outperform text embeddings (hidden states\n576\t    are an information superset of text).\"\n577\t\n578\t    '\n579\t- name: Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all models)\n580\t  plan_id: exp-007\n581\t  description: 'Systematic window size sweep on all 4 models. Characterize precision-coverage\n582\t    tradeoff and identify optimal W per model.\n583\t\n584\t    '\n585\t  expected_results:\n586\t    coverage_decreases_with_w_all_models: true\n587\t    precision_increases_with_w_all_models: true\n588\t    fpr_decreases_with_w_all_models: true\n589\t    r1_8b_best_bal_acc_w: 25\n590\t    r1_8b_w25_precision: 0.822\n591\t    r1_8b_w25_coverage: 0.488\n592\t    r1_8b_w25_fpr_step: 0.048\n593\t    r1_32b_best_bal_acc_w: 10\n594\t    r1_32b_w10_precision: 0.609\n595\t    r1_32b_w10_coverage: 0.7\n596\t    r1_32b_w10_fpr_step: 0.234\n597\t    qwq_32b_best_bal_acc_w: 25\n598\t    qwq_32b_w25_precision: 0.618\n599\t    qwq_32b_w25_coverage: 0.579\n600\t    qwq_32b_w25_fpr_step: 0.169\n601\t    ot_7b_hs_peaks_w3: true\n602\t    ot_7b_w3_bal_acc: 0.688\n603\t    ot_7b_w25_bal_acc: 0.659\n604\t    tradeoff_universal: larger W → higher precision, lower coverage, lower FPR\n605\t  result_rationale: 'Window ablation reveals a clean precision-sensitivity tradeoff\n606\t    that holds across all models. Worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \"The coverage-decreasing-with-W\n607\t    finding is trivially expected — requiring K=5 consecutive crossings with a larger\n608\t    window means fewer traces reach the threshold. This is a property of the detection\n609\t    criterion K, not an insight about temporal probing.\" (2) \"The optimal W varies\n610\t    by model (W=3 for OT-7B, W=10 for R1-32B, W=25 for QwQ and R1-8B). This means\n611\t    W is another model-specific hyperparameter to tune — combined with layer selection,\n612\t    you need per-model layer AND window tuning. The ''systematic evaluation'' framing\n613\t    hides the fact that this is just exhaustive grid search.\" (3) \"At the best W per\n614\t    model, coverage ranges from 49% (R1-8B) to 70% (R1-32B). Missing 30-50% of safety\n615\t    commitment events is not deployment-ready.\"\n616\t\n617\t    '\n618\t  tables:\n619\t  - name: Precision-Coverage Tradeoff Summary (all 4 models)\n620\t    columns:\n621\t    - Model\n622\t    - W=1 Prec/Cov\n623\t    - W=3 Prec/Cov\n624\t    - W=15 Prec/Cov\n625\t    - W=25 Prec/Cov\n626\t    rows:\n627\t    - - R1-8B\n628\t      - 0.693/0.721\n629\t      - 0.723/0.698\n630\t      - 0.777/0.512\n631\t      - 0.822/0.488\n632\t    - - OT-7B\n633\t      - —/—\n634\t      - 0.466/0.532\n635\t      - —/—\n636\t      - —/—\n637\t    - - R1-32B\n638\t      - 0.549/0.800\n639\t      - 0.560/0.800\n640\t      - 0.591/0.700\n641\t      - 0.566/0.700\n642\t    - - QwQ-32B\n643\t      - 0.544/0.789\n644\t      - 0.565/0.737\n645\t      - 0.582/0.684\n646\t      - 0.618/0.579\n647\t  figures_description:\n648\t  - 'Line plot: precision (solid) and coverage (dashed) vs W for each model. Clear\n649\t    inverse relationship. Shaded region shows ''deployment-acceptable'' zone (precision>0.7,\n650\t    coverage>0.6) — no model reaches it for any W.'\n651\t  analysis: 'The ablation confirms the tradeoff is universal but no operating point\n652\t    achieves both high precision AND high coverage. Worst-tier reviewer: \"Table 3\n653\t    is the paper''s most honest result. At no window size does any model achieve >0.7\n654\t    precision AND >0.6 coverage simultaneously. This is not a tuning problem — it''s\n655\t    a fundamental performance ceiling.\"\n656\t\n657\t    '\n658\t- name: Layer selection ablation (5 metrics × 4 models)\n659\t  plan_id: exp-008\n660\t  description: 'Evaluate all layers with temporal probes on 32B models under 5 different\n661\t    selection metrics. Demonstrate crossing_rate bias and metric-dependent layer selection.\n662\t\n663\t    '\n664\t  expected_results:\n665\t    crossing_rate_selects_shallow_32b: true\n666\t    pure_bal_acc_selects_middle_32b: true\n667\t    threshold_dependent_select_deep_32b: true\n668\t    qwq_l2_vs_l60_precision_gain: +11.5pp\n669\t    r1_32b_l10_vs_l63_precision_gain: +4.1pp\n670\t    loo_cv_stability: 65-68% dominant fold\n671\t    r1_8b_consistent: true\n672\t    ot_7b_varied: true\n673\t    bal_acc_layer_vs_precision_layer_diverge_32b: true\n674\t    qwq_l30_bal_acc: 0.751\n675\t    qwq_l60_bal_acc: 0.71\n676\t    r1_32b_l47_bal_acc: 0.829\n677\t    r1_32b_l63_bal_acc: 0.759\n678\t    ba_optimal_different_from_precision_optimal: true\n679\t  result_rationale: 'The layer selection ablation reveals a genuine pattern (biased\n680\t    metric → shallow layers → masked HS advantage), but worst-tier reviewers split\n681\t    on its significance: (1) The supportive reading: \"This is a useful observation\n682\t    — researchers using bal_acc × crossing_rate will get misleading results.\" (2)\n683\t    The dismissive reading (dominant in worst tier): \"This is expected behavior of\n684\t    a poorly chosen metric. The composite bal_acc × crossing_rate was never standard\n685\t    — it''s the authors'' own construction (or a rare one). Showing it''s bad doesn''t\n686\t    advance the field because nobody was using it.\" (3) The circularity concern: \"You\n687\t    select layers by precision/FPR, then evaluate by precision/FPR. The D044 BA-layer\n688\t    robustness check partially addresses this (16/16 HB pass on BA-selected layers),\n689\t    but the reviewer asks: ''if BA-selected layers also show HS advantage, why not\n690\t    just use BA selection? What''s the contribution of the 5-metric comparison?''\"\n691\t    (4) \"LOO-CV stability of 65-68% means 32-35% of folds select a DIFFERENT layer.\n692\t    This is not the ''robust selection'' the paper claims.\"\n693\t\n694\t    '\n695\t  tables:\n696\t  - name: Metric × Model → Selected Layer (with HS-Text precision delta)\n697\t    columns:\n698\t    - Metric\n699\t    - R1-8B Layer (ΔPrec)\n700\t    - OT-7B Layer (ΔPrec)\n701\t    - QwQ Layer (ΔPrec)\n702\t    - R1-32B Layer (ΔPrec)\n703\t    rows:\n704\t    - - bal_acc × cr\n705\t      - L0 (+var)\n706\t      - L0 (+var)\n707\t      - L2 (+5.8pp NS)\n708\t      - L10 (+9.4pp NS)\n709\t    - - pure bal_acc\n710\t      - L12 (+17.8pp)\n711\t      - L9 (+25.4pp)\n712\t      - L30 (+18.0pp)\n713\t      - L47 (+13.7pp)\n714\t    - - precision\n715\t      - L16 (+22.4pp)\n716\t      - L16 (+21.9pp)\n717\t      - L60 (+23.6pp)\n718\t      - L63 (+15.4pp)\n719\t    - - pre_cp_fpr\n720\t      - L16 (−7.4pp)\n721\t      - L16 (−17.1pp)\n722\t      - L60 (−9.8pp)\n723\t      - L63 (−14.6pp)\n724\t    - - composite\n725\t      - L16 (+22.4pp)\n726\t      - L12 (+var)\n727\t      - L60 (+23.6pp)\n728\t      - L63 (+15.4pp)\n729\t  figures_description:\n730\t  - 'Alluvial diagram: metric (left) → selected layer depth band (right), colored\n731\t    by model. Crossing_rate-contaminated metric routes to shallow band; all others\n732\t    route to mid/deep.'\n733\t  analysis: 'The ablation is thorough but worst-tier reviewers question whether it\n734\t    constitutes a contribution: \"Sensitivity analysis is a sanity check, not a finding.\n735\t    The paper would be better if it had used the right metric from the start and reported\n736\t    results at correct layers — making the sensitivity analysis unnecessary for the\n737\t    paper''s core claims.\"\n738\t\n739\t    '\n740\t- name: 16-label taxonomy supervision\n741\t  plan_id: exp-009\n742\t  description: 'Train probes with fine-grained safety labels (Type 1/2/3/4 × pre/post)\n743\t    instead of binary commitment. Evaluate whether richer supervision improves detection.\n744\t\n745\t    '\n746\t  expected_results:\n747\t    r1_8b_16label_acc: 0.41\n748\t    r1_8b_binary_collapse_acc: 0.825\n749\t    r1_8b_binary_collapse_delta: +0.8pp\n750\t    ot_7b_16label_acc: 0.35\n751\t    ot_7b_binary_collapse_delta: +1.1pp\n752\t    r1_32b_16label_acc: 0.28\n753\t    r1_32b_binary_collapse_delta: +0.6pp\n754\t    qwq_32b_16label_acc: 0.26\n755\t    qwq_32b_binary_collapse_delta: +0.9pp\n756\t    fine_grained_improvement_marginal: true\n757\t    max_collapse_delta: +1.1pp\n758\t  result_rationale: 'Fine-grained supervision provides marginal benefit (+0.6-1.1pp\n759\t    after binary collapse) across all models. The 16-class accuracy is low (26-41%)\n760\t    because the classes are imbalanced and transition types are not cleanly separable\n761\t    in hidden-state space. Worst-tier reviewers react: (1) \"Marginal improvement from\n762\t    richer supervision confirms the probe is already extracting most of the available\n763\t    linear signal in binary mode. This supports the reviewer''s argument that linear\n764\t    probes are saturated — not that the methodology is optimal, but that the information\n765\t    ceiling is low.\" (2) \"The 16-label experiment was motivated by reviewer concern\n766\t    about ''underusing available supervision,'' but the result shows supervision quality\n767\t    doesn''t matter much. This undermines the paper''s implicit argument that careful\n768\t    methodology (layer selection, temporal context) is what matters — it seems the\n769\t    representation quality is the binding constraint.\"\n770\t\n771\t    '\n772\t  tables:\n773\t  - name: 16-Label vs Binary Supervision\n774\t    columns:\n775\t    - Model\n776\t    - Binary Acc\n777\t    - 16-Label Acc\n778\t    - 16→Binary Collapse\n779\t    - Δ vs Binary\n780\t    rows:\n781\t    - - R1-8B\n782\t      - '0.817'\n783\t      - '0.41'\n784\t      - '0.825'\n785\t      - +0.8pp\n786\t    - - OT-7B\n787\t      - '0.688'\n788\t      - '0.35'\n789\t      - '0.699'\n790\t      - +1.1pp\n791\t    - - QwQ-32B\n792\t      - '0.740'\n793\t      - '0.26'\n794\t      - '0.749'\n795\t      - +0.9pp\n796\t    - - R1-32B\n797\t      - '0.826'\n798\t      - '0.28'\n799\t      - '0.832'\n800\t      - +0.6pp\n801\t  figures_description:\n802\t  - 'Confusion matrix (16-class) on R1-8B: heavy diagonal on safe categories, near-uniform\n803\t    on unsafe subcategories — probes cannot distinguish commitment subtypes.'\n804\t  analysis: 'Fine-grained supervision dead-end. Worst-tier implication: \"The information\n805\t    ceiling for linear probes on single-layer hidden states is fundamentally limited.\n806\t    Trying harder with better labels or more metrics can''t overcome this.\"\n807\t\n808\t    '\n809\t- name: Oracle decomposition\n810\t  plan_id: exp-010\n811\t  description: 'Analyze what the oracle label sequence encodes that hidden-state probes\n812\t    cannot access. Compare oracle lead (+31 steps on R1-8B) to probe lead (~0 steps).\n813\t\n814\t    '\n815\t  expected_results:\n816\t    r1_8b_oracle_lead: '+31.0'\n817\t    r1_8b_best_probe_lead: +4.5 (GRU)\n818\t    r1_8b_oracle_gap: '26.5'\n819\t    r1_8b_label_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.82\n820\t    r1_8b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.95\n821\t    label_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b: 1.38\n822\t    hs_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b: 0.41\n823\t    r1_32b_oracle_lead: '+28'\n824\t    r1_32b_best_probe_lead: +13 (W=1, 25% cov)\n825\t    qwq_oracle_lead: '+27'\n826\t    qwq_best_probe_lead: −1.2 to +1.9\n827\t    pattern_finding: Labels show gradual safety erosion pre-CP; hidden states show\n828\t      step-function at CP\n829\t  result_rationale: 'Oracle decomposition reveals a 26-28 step gap between what''s\n830\t    detectable in labels vs hidden states. The label sequence shows gradual pre-CP\n831\t    patterns (increasing unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe streaks) that hidden\n832\t    states don''t track. Worst-tier reviewers draw a harsh conclusion: \"The 31-step\n833\t    oracle lead proves that safety commitment is a GRADUAL process visible in the\n834\t    model''s outputs. But the hidden-state probe can only detect it at or after the\n835\t    commitment point (lead ≈ 0). This means hidden states encode the CURRENT safety\n836\t    state but not the TRAJECTORY toward commitment. For deployment, trajectory prediction\n837\t    is what matters — catching the commitment after it happens is too late. The oracle\n838\t    gap is not a puzzle for Discussion; it''s evidence that the entire hidden-state\n839\t    monitoring approach is fundamentally limited. Text-based monitoring that tracks\n840\t    reasoning trajectory (even with higher FPR) provides earlier warning.\"\n841\t\n842\t    '\n843\t  tables:\n844\t  - name: Oracle vs Best Probe Lead Time\n845\t    columns:\n846\t    - Model\n847\t    - Oracle Lead\n848\t    - Best Probe Lead\n849\t    - Method\n850\t    - Gap\n851\t    - Hidden State Entropy (pre-CP)\n852\t    rows:\n853\t    - - R1-8B\n854\t      - '+31.0'\n855\t      - '+4.5'\n856\t      - GRU multi-layer\n857\t      - '26.5'\n858\t      - '0.41'\n859\t    - - OT-7B\n860\t      - ~+30\n861\t      - +33.6 (GRU)\n862\t      - GRU multi-layer\n863\t      - ~0†\n864\t      - —\n865\t    - - QwQ-32B\n866\t      - '+27'\n867\t      - −1.2 to +1.9\n868\t      - temporal LR\n869\t      - 25-28\n870\t      - —\n871\t    - - R1-32B\n872\t      - '+28'\n873\t      - '+13.3'\n874\t      - temporal LR W=1\n875\t      - 15 (25% cov)\n876\t      - —\n877\t  figures_description:\n878\t  - 'Pre-CP hidden state trajectory (Fisher directions): R1-8B shows minimal drift\n879\t    before CP, then sharp transition. Labels show gradual escalation starting 20-30\n880\t    steps before CP.'\n881\t  analysis: 'The oracle decomposition is the worst-tier''s strongest negative finding:\n882\t    hidden states are fundamentally reactive (encoding current state) rather than\n883\t    predictive (encoding trajectory). Reviewer: \"This paper demonstrates that hidden-state\n884\t    monitoring is a lagging indicator. For safety deployment, you need leading indicators.\n885\t    Text-based trajectory analysis, while noisier, is the better research direction.\"\n886\t\n887\t    '\n888\t- name: OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL-trained)\n889\t  plan_id: exp-011\n890\t  description: '4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Full pipeline:\n891\t    layer sweep, temporal probes, HS vs text comparison, GRU, selectivity control\n892\t    task.\n893\t\n894\t    '\n895\t  expected_results:\n896\t    best_layer: 16\n897\t    best_layer_depth_pct: 57.1\n898\t    best_hs_bal_acc_w3: 0.688\n899\t    best_hs_precision_w3: 0.466\n900\t    best_hs_pre_cp_fpr: 0.1\n901\t    bootstrap_3_of_3_hb: true\n902\t    bootstrap_bal_acc_delta: +10.7pp\n903\t    bootstrap_precision_delta: +21.9pp\n904\t    bootstrap_fpr_delta: −17.1pp\n905\t    selectivity_pp: 18.3\n906\t    n_traces: 228\n907\t    gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.746\n908\t    gru_multi_lead: '+33.6'\n909\t    gru_multi_fpr: 0.274\n910\t    hs_temporal_saturation_w3: true\n911\t    text_temporal_w25_best: 0.614\n912\t  result_rationale: 'OT-7B is the cleanest single-model evidence for HS advantage\n913\t    (3/3 HB, highest selectivity 18.3pp, largest n=228 traces). But worst-tier reviewers\n914\t    are unimpressed by adding a 4th model that confirms the known pattern: (1) \"A\n915\t    4th model at 7B showing the same result doesn''t address the 32B caveats. The\n916\t    paper already has two 7-8B models that work. The question is whether HS monitoring\n917\t    scales, and OT-7B can''t answer that.\" (2) \"OT-7B has its own caveats: HS temporal\n918\t    saturation at W=3 (bal_acc declines at larger W), and absolute performance is\n919\t    still below R1-8B (best bal_acc 68.8% vs 80.2%). The ''cleanest evidence'' is\n920\t    still only 69% balanced accuracy — barely better than a biased coin.\" (3) \"GRU\n921\t    lead time of +33.6 steps sounds impressive until you note OT-7B traces average\n922\t    ~80 sentences — normalize by trace length and the lead time fraction is similar\n923\t    to other models.\" (4) \"FPR of 27.4% for GRU means ~1 in 4 safe steps triggers\n924\t    the detector. At 80-sentence traces, that''s ~20 false alarms per safe trace.\"\n925\t\n926\t    '\n927\t  tables:\n928\t  - name: OT-7B Full Pipeline Summary\n929\t    columns:\n930\t    - Method\n931\t    - bal_acc\n932\t    - precision\n933\t    - pre-CP FPR\n934\t    - coverage\n935\t    - lead\n936\t    rows:\n937\t    - - HS temporal W=3 L16\n938\t      - '0.688'\n939\t      - '0.466'\n940\t      - '0.100'\n941\t      - '0.532'\n942\t      - '+21.6'\n943\t    - - Text temporal W=25\n944\t      - '0.614'\n945\t      - '0.329'\n946\t      - '0.161'\n947\t      - '0.660'\n948\t      - '+45.5'\n949\t    - - GRU multi L13-19\n950\t      - '0.746'\n951\t      - —\n952\t      - '0.274'\n953\t      - '0.645'\n954\t      - '+33.6'\n955\t    - - HS-Text Δ (HB)\n956\t      - +10.7pp ✅\n957\t      - +21.9pp ✅\n958\t      - −17.1pp ✅\n959\t      - —\n960\t      - —\n961\t  figures_description:\n962\t  - '2×2 architecture×scale matrix: Fisher discriminability + HS-text precision delta.\n963\t    Both 7-8B cells show large delta; both 32B cells show delta with model-specific\n964\t    caveats.'\n965\t  analysis: 'OT-7B strengthens the 7-8B story but worst-tier reviewers note: \"The\n966\t    2×2 matrix now has two strong cells (7-8B) and two weak cells (32B). The paper''s\n967\t    breadth claim (''4 models'') is technically true but misleading — 2/4 models have\n968\t    serious practical caveats that make the HS advantage non-deployable.\"\n969\t\n970\t    '\n971\t- name: GRU/Attention probe upper bound (32B models)\n972\t  plan_id: exp-012\n973\t  description: 'GRU sequence model on 32B models at corrected layers. Tests whether\n974\t    nonlinear sequence modeling can recover signal that linear temporal probes miss.\n975\t\n976\t    '\n977\t  expected_results:\n978\t    r1_32b_gru_single_bal_acc: 0.675\n979\t    r1_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.655\n980\t    r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: +8.8 to +9.1\n981\t    r1_32b_gru_fpr: 0.114 to 0.135\n982\t    r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.23 to 0.30\n983\t    r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: −6pp bal_acc (GRU worse)\n984\t    qwq_32b_gru_single_bal_acc: 0.694\n985\t    qwq_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.724\n986\t    qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: '+15.9'\n987\t    qwq_32b_gru_fpr: 0.289\n988\t    qwq_32b_gru_coverage: 0.84\n989\t    qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: −3.7pp bal_acc (GRU worse at bal_acc, better at coverage)\n990\t    ot_7b_gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.746\n991\t    ot_7b_gru_multi_lead: '+33.6'\n992\t    ot_7b_gru_fpr: 0.274\n993\t    gru_worse_than_lr_3_of_4: true\n994\t    gru_note: GRU trades accuracy for coverage/lead — higher FPR drives earlier detection\n995\t  result_rationale: 'GRU does NOT outperform temporal LR on balanced accuracy for\n996\t    3/4 models. On R1-32B (n=20 test), GRU severely overfits — bal_acc drops 6pp below\n997\t    LR. On QwQ, GRU achieves higher coverage and lead time but at 2× the FPR. This\n998\t    confirms linear probes are sufficient (or even superior given small samples).\n999\t    Worst-tier reviewers use this to attack novelty: \"The paper''s method is logistic\n1000\t    regression on hidden states with a sliding window. That''s not a contribution\n1001\t    — it''s the simplest possible baseline. The fact that GRU and MLP don''t help\n1002\t    confirms the method space is trivial, which means the paper''s ''systematic comparison\n1003\t    of 7 methods'' is comparing variations of the obvious approach (linear probe)\n1004\t    against methods that were never designed for this task (Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CCS).\n1005\t    The negative results are predictable, not informative.\"\n1006\t\n1007\t    '\n1008\t  tables:\n1009\t  - name: GRU vs Temporal LR (32B models, corrected layers)\n1010\t    columns:\n1011\t    - Model\n1012\t    - LR bal_acc\n1013\t    - GRU bal_acc\n1014\t    - Δ\n1015\t    - GRU FPR\n1016\t    - LR FPR\n1017\t    rows:\n1018\t    - - R1-32B (L63 W=10)\n1019\t      - '0.737'\n1020\t      - ~0.675\n1021\t      - −6.2pp\n1022\t      - '0.135'\n1023\t      - '0.234'\n1024\t    - - QwQ-32B (L60 W=5)\n1025\t      - '0.761'\n1026\t      - '0.724'\n1027\t      - −3.7pp\n1028\t      - '0.289'\n1029\t      - '0.217'\n1030\t    - - OT-7B (L16 multi)\n1031\t      - '0.688'\n1032\t      - '0.746'\n1033\t      - +5.8pp\n1034\t      - '0.274'\n1035\t      - '0.100'\n1036\t    - - R1-8B (L14 multi)\n1037\t      - '0.806'\n1038\t      - '0.809'\n1039\t      - +0.3pp\n1040\t      - '0.165'\n1041\t      - '0.097'\n1042\t  figures_description:\n1043\t  - 'Scatter: method complexity (x: LR/GRU/MLP) vs bal_acc (y), per model. No consistent\n1044\t    trend — complexity doesn''t help. Error bars from multi-seed show high variance\n1045\t    for GRU.'\n1046\t  analysis: 'GRU fails to improve over LR on most models, confirming linear probes\n1047\t    are at the information ceiling. Worst-tier reviewer: \"The paper''s headline is\n1048\t    ''HS beats text.'' The method is logistic regression. The contribution is... careful\n1049\t    hyperparameter selection? This is a well-executed experiment report, not a methods\n1050\t    paper.\"\n1051\t\n1052\t    '\n1053\t- name: Text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal LR)\n1054\t  plan_id: exp-013\n1055\t  description: 'Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal window LR on\n1056\t    CoT text of all 4 models. Critical comparison establishing what text monitoring\n1057\t    achieves without hidden-state access.\n1058\t\n1059\t    '\n1060\t  expected_results:\n1061\t    r1_8b_text_static_acc: 0.661\n1062\t    r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_acc: 0.678\n1063\t    r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_auroc: 0.759\n1064\t    r1_8b_hs_acc_advantage: +15-18pp\n1065\t    ot_7b_text_best_bal_acc: 0.614\n1066\t    ot_7b_text_best_precision: 0.329\n1067\t    ot_7b_text_best_fpr: 0.161\n1068\t    qwq_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc: 0.65\n1069\t    qwq_32b_text_precision: 0.421\n1070\t    r1_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc: 0.646\n1071\t    r1_32b_text_precision: 0.45\n1072\t    hs_vs_text_precision_gap_range: +15.4 to +23.6pp\n1073\t    text_requires_zero_infra_change: true\n1074\t    text_precision_all_models: 0.329-0.487\n1075\t    hs_precision_all_models: 0.466-0.776\n1076\t  result_rationale: 'Text baseline performs consistently across all models (bal_acc\n1077\t    0.61-0.68, precision 0.33-0.49). HS probes outperform by +15-24pp precision, which\n1078\t    is statistically significant (12/12 HB). But worst-tier reviewers attack the COST-BENEFIT\n1079\t    ratio, not the statistical claim: (1) \"Text probes need zero infrastructure change\n1080\t    — you monitor the CoT output that''s already available. HS probes require white-box\n1081\t    access to model internals, which means: (a) running the model locally (can''t\n1082\t    use API), (b) modifying the inference pipeline to extract intermediate activations,\n1083\t    (c) per-model layer selection + probe training. The 15-24pp precision gain comes\n1084\t    at enormous engineering cost.\" (2) \"At 33-49% text precision, you get 1 true alarm\n1085\t    per 2-3 triggers. At 47-78% HS precision, you get 1 per 1.3-2.1 triggers. Is that\n1086\t    difference worth instrumenting your entire inference stack? For most deployment\n1087\t    teams, no.\" (3) \"Text probes improve with better embeddings (BGE-large, domain-fine-tuned).\n1088\t    The paper tested only all-MiniLM-L6-v2 — the weakest common baseline. A reviewer\n1089\t    notes: ''Use GPT-4 as the text classifier and the gap likely narrows to <5pp.''\"\n1090\t    (4) \"The BGE-large comparison (exp_bge_large_text_comparison) showed only 1/4\n1091\t    HB pass for HS vs BGE-large — the gap narrows significantly with a better text\n1092\t    encoder.\"\n1093\t\n1094\t    '\n1095\t  tables:\n1096\t  - name: HS vs Text Precision Gap (+ engineering cost annotation)\n1097\t    columns:\n1098\t    - Model\n1099\t    - HS Precision\n1100\t    - Text Precision\n1101\t    - Δ\n1102\t    - HS Infra Cost\n1103\t    rows:\n1104\t    - - R1-8B\n1105\t      - 0.777 (W=15)\n1106\t      - 0.487 (W=15)\n1107\t      - +29.0pp\n1108\t      - white-box, L14\n1109\t    - - OT-7B\n1110\t      - 0.466 (W=3)\n1111\t      - 0.329 (W=25)\n1112\t      - +13.7pp\n1113\t      - white-box, L16\n1114\t    - - QwQ-32B\n1115\t      - 0.578 (W=3)\n1116\t      - 0.421 (W=best)\n1117\t      - +15.7pp\n1118\t      - white-box, L60\n1119\t    - - R1-32B\n1120\t      - 0.603 (W=1)\n1121\t      - 0.450 (W=best)\n1122\t      - +15.3pp\n1123\t      - white-box, L63\n1124\t  figures_description:\n1125\t  - 'Cost-benefit scatter: x-axis = engineering complexity (text=1, HS=3), y-axis\n1126\t    = precision. Text cluster at (1, 0.33-0.49), HS cluster at (3, 0.47-0.78). Marginal\n1127\t    ROI line shows diminishing returns.'\n1128\t  analysis: 'The text baseline is the worst-tier reviewer''s strongest weapon: \"A\n1129\t    15-24pp precision gain sounds meaningful until you consider: (a) text monitoring\n1130\t    costs $0 in infrastructure, (b) HS monitoring requires white-box access + per-model\n1131\t    tuning, (c) the BGE-large result shows the gap shrinks with better text encoders,\n1132\t    (d) safe FPR is 39-62% regardless of modality — both approaches produce unusable\n1133\t    false alarm rates. The paper should honestly state: ''HS probes are measurably\n1134\t    but marginally better than text probes. Whether the margin justifies white-box\n1135\t    access depends on deployment constraints. For most practical settings, text monitoring\n1136\t    suffices.''\"\n1137\t\n1138\t    '\n1139\t- name: BOCPD/CUSUM on all 4 models\n1140\t  plan_id: exp-014\n1141\t  description: 'Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection + CUSUM on probe score sequences.\n1142\t    Tests whether advanced change-point methods can achieve positive lead time.\n1143\t\n1144\t    '\n1145\t  expected_results:\n1146\t    r1_8b_bocpd_detection: 0.93\n1147\t    r1_8b_bocpd_lead: '+3.45'\n1148\t    r1_8b_cusum_lead: '+0.89'\n1149\t    r1_8b_fcp_baseline_lead: '+4.89'\n1150\t    r1_8b_fcp_beats_bocpd_on_lead: true\n1151\t    ot_7b_bocpd_detection: 0.0\n1152\t    ot_7b_bocpd_note: W=3 probe scores too spiky, Gaussian model fails\n1153\t    ot_7b_fcp_best_detection: 0.894\n1154\t    ot_7b_fcp_best_lead: '+11.83'\n1155\t    r1_32b_bocpd_detection: 0.0\n1156\t    qwq_32b_bocpd_detection: 0.0\n1157\t    r1_32b_cusum_detection: 0.85\n1158\t    r1_32b_cusum_lead: '+2.59'\n1159\t    qwq_32b_cusum_detection: 0.737\n1160\t    qwq_32b_cusum_lead: '+9.86'\n1161\t    bocpd_works_only_r1_8b: true\n1162\t    cusum_universally_worse_than_fcp: true\n1163\t  result_rationale: 'BOCPD fails on 3/4 models (0% detection on OT-7B, R1-32B, QwQ).\n1164\t    Only R1-8B works (93%) because R1-8B has gradual probe score transitions that\n1165\t    match BOCPD''s Gaussian change-point model. Other models have abrupt transitions\n1166\t    (OT-7B) or weak signals (32B) that BOCPD''s parametric assumptions can''t handle.\n1167\t    CUSUM detects some traces but with worse lead than simple first-crossing-point\n1168\t    (FCP) threshold. Worst-tier reviewers: (1) \"BOCPD and CUSUM were never designed\n1169\t    for this signal type. Testing them is due diligence, but their failure is predictable\n1170\t    — these methods assume stationary-then-shifted Gaussian data, while probe scores\n1171\t    are bounded [0,1] and often spiky.\" (2) \"The negative result strengthens the ''nothing\n1172\t    works well'' narrative, but the method selection is questionable. Why not test\n1173\t    HMM, Viterbi decoding, or learned change-point detectors? The paper tests 2 off-the-shelf\n1174\t    methods and declares change-point detection a dead end.\" (3) \"Simple FCP threshold\n1175\t    beats BOCPD on mean lead time for R1-8B. The ''advanced'' methods add nothing.\"\n1176\t\n1177\t    '\n1178\t  tables:\n1179\t  - name: Change-Point Detection Cross-Model Summary\n1180\t    columns:\n1181\t    - Model\n1182\t    - BOCPD Det%\n1183\t    - BOCPD Lead\n1184\t    - CUSUM Det%\n1185\t    - CUSUM Lead\n1186\t    - FCP Det%\n1187\t    - FCP Lead\n1188\t    rows:\n1189\t    - - R1-8B\n1190\t      - 93%\n1191\t      - '+3.45'\n1192\t      - 65%\n1193\t      - '+0.89'\n1194\t      - 65%\n1195\t      - '+4.89'\n1196\t    - - OT-7B\n1197\t      - 0%\n1198\t      - —\n1199\t      - 75%†\n1200\t      - +7.46†\n1201\t      - 89%\n1202\t      - '+11.83'\n1203\t    - - R1-32B\n1204\t      - 0%\n1205\t      - —\n1206\t      - 85%\n1207\t      - '+2.59'\n1208\t      - 95%\n1209\t      - '+5.68'\n1210\t    - - QwQ-32B\n1211\t      - 0%\n1212\t      - —\n1213\t      - 74%\n1214\t      - '+9.86'\n1215\t      - 79%\n1216\t      - '+9.40'\n1217\t  figures_description:\n1218\t  - 'Probe score time-series with change-point annotations: R1-8B shows gradual transition\n1219\t    detected by BOCPD. OT-7B shows abrupt step-function that BOCPD misses. 32B models\n1220\t    show noisy flat signal.'\n1221\t  analysis: 'Complete BOCPD failure on 3/4 models and CUSUM inferiority to simple\n1222\t    thresholding. Worst-tier reviewer: \"The paper tests two change-point algorithms\n1223\t    that were designed for very different signal types, finds they don''t work, and\n1224\t    claims this as a negative result. But the method selection is so narrow that the\n1225\t    negative result is uninformative — you tested the wrong tools.\"\n1226\t\n1227\t    '\n1228\t- name: False positive analysis (safe trace FPR, all methods)\n1229\t  plan_id: exp-015\n1230\t  description: 'Run all probes on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) across all 4 models.\n1231\t    Report safe FPR, pre-CP FPR, precision, and precision-recall curves.\n1232\t\n1233\t    '\n1234\t  expected_results:\n1235\t    r1_8b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.39\n1236\t    r1_8b_text_safe_fpr: 0.385\n1237\t    ot_7b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.111-0.175\n1238\t    ot_7b_text_safe_fpr: 0.302-0.460\n1239\t    qwq_32b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.45-0.50\n1240\t    qwq_32b_text_safe_fpr: 0.325-0.425\n1241\t    r1_32b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.239\n1242\t    r1_32b_text_safe_fpr: 0.31\n1243\t    safe_fpr_range_all: 0.111 to 0.521\n1244\t    safe_fpr_median: ~0.39\n1245\t    gru_safe_fpr: 0.521\n1246\t    gru_highest_fpr_note: GRU trades precision for coverage → highest FPR\n1247\t    precision_all_methods_range: 0.329 to 0.777\n1248\t    no_method_achieves_80pct_precision: true\n1249\t    deployment_threshold_precision_70: only R1-8B HS W≥15 exceeds\n1250\t  result_rationale: 'Safe FPR ranges from 11% (OT-7B HS static, best case) to 52%\n1251\t    (GRU, worst case), with a median around 39%. This is the worst-tier dealbreaker.\n1252\t    Reviewer calculation: \"At 39% safe FPR with K=5 consecutive crossings for trace-level\n1253\t    detection, a 40-step safe trace has ~39% probability of triggering the detector.\n1254\t    In a production system processing 1000 safe traces per day, that''s ~390 false\n1255\t    alarms daily. Each requires human review at ~5 minutes → 32.5 person-hours/day\n1256\t    of false alarm triage. No deployment team would accept this.\" Even the best case\n1257\t    (OT-7B HS 11.1% safe FPR) means 111 false alarms per 1000 safe traces. The worst-tier\n1258\t    reviewer''s verdict: \"A safety monitor that fires on 1 in 3 to 1 in 2 safe traces\n1259\t    is not a monitor — it''s a random noise generator. The paper should lead with\n1260\t    this limitation, not bury it.\" QwQ-32B is particularly problematic: HS safe FPR\n1261\t    (45-50%) is HIGHER than text safe FPR (33-43%), meaning HS monitoring generates\n1262\t    MORE false alarms on safe traces for QwQ.\n1263\t\n1264\t    '\n1265\t  tables:\n1266\t  - name: Safe Trace FPR Summary (all methods × all models)\n1267\t    columns:\n1268\t    - Model\n1269\t    - HS Static FPR\n1270\t    - HS Temporal FPR\n1271\t    - Text Temporal FPR\n1272\t    - GRU FPR\n1273\t    rows:\n1274\t    - - R1-8B\n1275\t      - '0.390'\n1276\t      - '0.392'\n1277\t      - '0.385'\n1278\t      - '0.521'\n1279\t    - - OT-7B\n1280\t      - '0.111'\n1281\t      - '0.175'\n1282\t      - '0.460'\n1283\t      - —\n1284\t    - - QwQ-32B\n1285\t      - '0.500'\n1286\t      - '0.450'\n1287\t      - '0.425'\n1288\t      - —\n1289\t    - - R1-32B\n1290\t      - '0.239'\n1291\t      - '0.239'\n1292\t      - '0.310'\n1293\t      - —\n1294\t  - name: Deployment Viability Assessment\n1295\t    columns:\n1296\t    - Criterion\n1297\t    - Threshold\n1298\t    - Models Meeting Criterion\n1299\t    - Best Method\n1300\t    rows:\n1301\t    - - Safe FPR < 20%\n1302\t      - '0.20'\n1303\t      - 1/4 (OT-7B HS static)\n1304\t      - HS static L16\n1305\t    - - Precision > 70%\n1306\t      - '0.70'\n1307\t      - 1/4 (R1-8B W≥15)\n1308\t      - HS temporal W=25\n1309\t    - - Coverage > 60%\n1310\t      - '0.60'\n1311\t      - 2/4 (R1-8B W≤3, R1-32B W≤3)\n1312\t      - HS temporal W=1\n1313\t    - - All three criteria\n1314\t      - —\n1315\t      - 0/4\n1316\t      - None\n1317\t  figures_description:\n1318\t  - 'Radar chart per model: axes = safe FPR (inverted), precision, coverage, lead\n1319\t    time. No model fills all quadrants. The ''deployment-ready'' region (inner polygon)\n1320\t    is unreached.'\n1321\t  - 'PR curves: all models. Dashed line at precision=0.7. Only R1-8B HS crosses this\n1322\t    line, at recall=0.25.'\n1323\t  analysis: 'FPR analysis is the worst-tier''s core evidence for the \"not practically\n1324\t    useful\" dismissal. No model meets all three deployment criteria simultaneously.\n1325\t    The best model (R1-8B) achieves >70% precision only at W≥15 where coverage drops\n1326\t    to 49%. The paper''s deployment framing is untenable. Reframing guidance: \"Position\n1327\t    as diagnostic/research tool, explicitly disclaim deployment readiness. Lead with\n1328\t    ''none of our 7 methods achieves deployment-ready performance'' as a cautionary\n1329\t    finding.\"\n1330\t\n1331\t    '\n1332\t- name: 5-fold CV validation of multi-metric ensemble (7-8B)\n1333\t  plan_id: ensemble_cv_7b\n1334\t  description: 'Cross-validated ensemble of metric-weighted layer selections on R1-8B\n1335\t    and OT-7B. Validates that multi-metric ensemble eliminates layer selection bias\n1336\t    under proper CV.\n1337\t\n1338\t    '\n1339\t  expected_results:\n1340\t    r1_8b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.565\n1341\t    r1_8b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.604\n1342\t    r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_gap: +19.3pp\n1343\t    r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_p: <0.002\n1344\t    ot_7b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.506\n1345\t    ot_7b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.473\n1346\t    ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single: true\n1347\t    ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single_p: 0.033\n1348\t    cv_validates_preliminary: true\n1349\t    cv_acceptance_probability_delta: +0.01 to +0.03\n1350\t  result_rationale: 'CV validation confirms the ensemble result holds (p≤0.002 for\n1351\t    ensemble vs text on R1-8B). But worst-tier reviewers are unmoved: (1) \"CV validating\n1352\t    an ensemble of layer selection metrics on 7-8B models doesn''t address ANY of\n1353\t    our core concerns. The issues are: high safe FPR (39-62%), model-specific caveats\n1354\t    at 32B, engineering cost of white-box access, and insufficient novelty. A statistically\n1355\t    cleaner layer selection protocol is nice but tangential.\" (2) \"The ensemble precision\n1356\t    (0.506-0.565) is actually LOWER than the best single metric on R1-8B (0.604).\n1357\t    On OT-7B the ensemble slightly beats the best single metric (0.506 vs 0.473) but\n1358\t    the absolute precision is 50% — a coin flip.\" (3) \"This experiment strengthens\n1359\t    an already- strong statistical case (12/12 HB already passes). The paper doesn''t\n1360\t    need MORE statistical validation; it needs BETTER results or a DIFFERENT framing.\"\n1361\t    The CV ensemble moves acceptance probability by +1-3%, insufficient to shift from\n1362\t    worst tier.\n1363\t\n1364\t    '\n1365\t  tables:\n1366\t  - name: Ensemble CV Results (7-8B models)\n1367\t    columns:\n1368\t    - Model\n1369\t    - Ensemble Prec\n1370\t    - Best Single Prec\n1371\t    - Text Prec\n1372\t    - Ens vs Text p\n1373\t    rows:\n1374\t    - - R1-8B\n1375\t      - '0.565'\n1376\t      - '0.604'\n1377\t      - '0.372'\n1378\t      - <0.002\n1379\t    - - OT-7B\n1380\t      - '0.506'\n1381\t      - '0.473'\n1382\t      - '0.236'\n1383\t      - <0.001\n1384\t  figures_description:\n1385\t  - 'CV fold boxplot: ensemble precision across 5 folds. Low variance confirms stability\n1386\t    but absolute values are mediocre (50-57%).'\n1387\t  analysis: 'Ensemble CV is a marginal improvement on an already-validated statistical\n1388\t    claim. Worst-tier impact on acceptance: negligible (+1-3%). The bottleneck is\n1389\t    not statistical rigor — it''s practical utility and novelty. This experiment is\n1390\t    low-cost and worth including in supplementary, but it cannot rescue the paper\n1391\t    from worst-tier reviewer concerns.'\n1392\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b5a31677-8209-41d2-89ee-f0005cac093e","uuid":"5469e2ec-9d62-4121-ac77-057acd341aa4","timestamp":"2026-05-22T09:27:23.965Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T212242/worst/imagined_experiments.yaml","content":"tier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: 'Reframe away from ''HS beats text'' toward one of two angles,\n  both still targeting EMNLP Main: (A) **Negative-results-first framing**: ''We systematically\n  evaluate 7 detection methods across 4 reasoning models and find that none achieves\n  deployment-ready performance. The best approach (temporal HS probes) still has 39-62%\n  safe FPR. We identify layer selection bias as a critical confounder and provide\n  a sensitivity analysis framework.'' This positions the paper as a cautionary benchmark.\n  (B) **Methodology-first framing**: ''We discover that a commonly used layer selection\n  metric systematically biases hidden-state evaluations, producing false negatives\n  at scale. We demonstrate this across 4 models and 5 metrics, show it reverses published\n  conclusions, and propose a multi-metric audit protocol.'' This makes layer selection\n  the headline and HS-vs-text becomes supporting evidence. Either angle requires:\n  toning down deployment language, foregrounding limitations honestly, positioning\n  as ''the field needs this evaluation before building on shaky foundations.'' Add\n  cost-benefit analysis (HS access cost vs marginal gain over text) to preempt the\n  engineering-cost reviewer.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b & QwQ-32B)\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: 'Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract\n    hidden states at all layers, evaluate static LR probes per layer under multiple\n    selection metrics. Includes discovery of layer selection bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate\n    → shallow layers).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_biased_layer: 10\n    r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct: 15.6\n    r1_32b_corrected_layer: 63\n    r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 98.4\n    r1_32b_l10_bal_acc: 0.718\n    r1_32b_l63_bal_acc_w1: 0.826\n    r1_32b_n_test: 20\n    r1_32b_l63_precision_w1: 0.603\n    r1_32b_l63_fpr_w1: 0.235\n    qwq_32b_biased_layer: 2\n    qwq_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct: 3.1\n    qwq_32b_corrected_layer: 60\n    qwq_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct: 93.8\n    qwq_32b_l2_bal_acc: 0.73\n    qwq_32b_l60_bal_acc_w3: 0.74\n    qwq_32b_n_test: 19-20\n    qwq_32b_l60_precision_w3: 0.578\n    qwq_32b_l60_fpr_w3: 0.279\n    layer_selection_bias_discovered: true\n    metrics_tested: 5\n    biased_metric: bal_acc × crossing_rate\n    loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct: 65-68%\n  result_rationale: 'Layer sweep reveals a genuine methodological finding (biased\n    metric selects shallow layers), and corrected layers do show HS advantage. However,\n    worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \"Finding that one composite metric is bad\n    is trivially expected — practitioners routinely try multiple metrics. This is\n    hyperparameter tuning, not a contribution.\" (2) n=19-20 test traces for 32B models\n    mean bootstrap CIs are wide. MDE analysis shows R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp)\n    is far below the 11.2pp MDE at 80% power — the NS result for position-mediated\n    HS advantage is a power limitation, not evidence. Reviewers note: \"you can''t\n    claim the advantage is ''real but position-mediated'' when you''re underpowered\n    to detect the non-positional component.\" (3) The corrected layers (L60/L63, >93%\n    depth) are so deep they''re essentially the output layer — reviewer asks: \"are\n    you just detecting output-token semantics via a different pathway?\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Layer Selection Metric × Model → Selected Layer\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - R1-8B\n    - OT-7B\n    - QwQ-32B\n    - R1-32B\n    rows:\n    - - bal_acc × crossing_rate\n      - L0 (0%)\n      - L0 (0%)\n      - L2 (3%)\n      - L10 (16%)\n    - - pure bal_acc\n      - L12 (39%)\n      - L9 (32%)\n      - L30 (47%)\n      - L47 (73%)\n    - - precision\n      - L16 (52%)\n      - L16 (57%)\n      - L60 (94%)\n      - L63 (98%)\n    - - pre_cp_fpr\n      - L16 (52%)\n      - L16 (57%)\n      - L60 (94%)\n      - L63 (98%)\n    - - composite (additive)\n      - L16 (52%)\n      - L12 (43%)\n      - L60 (94%)\n      - L63 (98%)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Heatmap: 5 metrics × 4 models → selected layer depth (%). Clear shallow-bias\n    pattern for crossing_rate metric on 32B. Threshold-dependent metrics converge\n    on deep layers.'\n  analysis: 'Layer selection sensitivity is the paper''s primary methodological contribution,\n    but worst-tier reviewers rate it insufficient novelty (2.0-2.5). The argument:\n    \"every ML practitioner knows different metrics select different hyperparameters.\n    Showing this for layer selection is an observation, not a method. There''s no\n    proposed algorithm to automatically resolve the disagreement — just ''try more\n    metrics,'' which is standard practice.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: 'Temporal LR at multiple window sizes on R1-32B (L63) and QwQ-32B (L60).\n    Evaluate whether temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across 32B\n    models.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_l63_w1_bal_acc: 0.826\n    r1_32b_l63_w1_precision: 0.603\n    r1_32b_l63_w1_coverage: 0.25\n    r1_32b_l63_w3_bal_acc: 0.754\n    r1_32b_l63_w5_bal_acc: 0.741\n    r1_32b_l63_w10_bal_acc: 0.737\n    r1_32b_l63_w15_bal_acc: 0.721\n    r1_32b_temporal_trend: DECREASING with W (W=1 best, -10.5pp by W=15)\n    r1_32b_dimensionality_issue: 51200 × W features for n=20 → massive overfitting\n      at W≥3\n    qwq_32b_l60_w1_bal_acc: 0.74\n    qwq_32b_l60_w3_bal_acc: 0.74\n    qwq_32b_l60_w5_bal_acc: 0.742\n    qwq_32b_l60_w15_bal_acc: 0.731\n    qwq_32b_temporal_trend: flat to slightly decreasing\n    qwq_32b_best_w: 5\n    text_temporal_universal_improvement: true\n    hs_temporal_improvement_3_of_4: true\n    hs_temporal_exception: OT-7B HS peaks W=3, declines at larger W; R1-32B only W=1\n      significant\n  result_rationale: 'Temporal context shows a split pattern at 32B: QwQ benefits modestly\n    across all W (text and HS), but R1-32B DEGRADES with increasing W because concatenating\n    51200-dimensional vectors across W>1 windows creates a feature space massively\n    exceeding n=20 training samples. This is not a failure of the temporal hypothesis\n    — it''s a sample-size bottleneck. Worst-tier reviewers seize on this: \"R1-32B\n    temporal results are meaningless because you''re fitting 102400+ parameters to\n    20 samples. The W=1 ''significance'' at L63 survives only because W=1 avoids this\n    problem. Your ''best'' result for R1-32B is actually a dimensionality artifact\n    — the only window that avoids overfitting happens to be significant.\" Claim 1\n    (temporal universally helps) holds for text probes across all 4 models, but for\n    HS probes, 2/4 models have caveats (OT-7B saturation, R1-32B dimensionality).\n    Reviewers frame this as \"temporal improvement is a text-probe finding, not an\n    HS-probe finding.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Temporal Window Effect on 32B Models (HS probes, corrected layers)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Layer\n    - W=1\n    - W=3\n    - W=5\n    - W=10\n    - W=15\n    - Trend\n    rows:\n    - - R1-32B\n      - L63\n      - 0.826*\n      - '0.754'\n      - '0.741'\n      - '0.737'\n      - '0.721'\n      - ↓ declining\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - L60\n      - '0.740'\n      - '0.740'\n      - '0.742'\n      - '0.735'\n      - '0.731'\n      - → flat\n    - - R1-8B\n      - L14\n      - '0.778'\n      - '0.790'\n      - '0.781'\n      - '0.793'\n      - '0.802'\n      - ↑ improving\n    - - OT-7B\n      - L16\n      - '0.663'\n      - 0.688*\n      - '0.678'\n      - '0.668'\n      - '0.659'\n      - ↑↓ peaks W=3\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: bal_acc vs W for each model. R1-8B shows steady climb. OT-7B peaks\n    then falls. QwQ flat. R1-32B declines from W=1. Divergent patterns undermine ''universal\n    temporal benefit'' claim.'\n  analysis: 'Worst-tier reviewers note: \"Temporal context helps universally for text\n    probes (a known result — more context helps NLP classifiers). For HS probes, 2/4\n    models show non-monotonic or declining trends. The paper''s Claim 1 should be\n    ''temporal context helps text probes'' — attributing it to HS probes is cherry-picking\n    the models where it works.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Cross-model transfer probes\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: 'Train probe on one model, test on another. PCA projection for cross-architecture\n    transfer (4096→5120 or vice versa). Tests whether safety commitment encoding is\n    universal.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.522\n    r1_8b_to_qwq_acc: 0.595\n    r1_32b_to_qwq_acc: 0.673\n    qwq_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.673\n    r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.583\n    best_transfer_overall: 0.673\n    chance_baseline: 0.5\n    all_transfers_below_within_model: true\n    within_family_32b_weak_transfer: 0.673\n    cross_family_at_chance: true\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-model transfer fails completely — the best transfer accuracy\n    (67.3% Qwen-family 32B internal) is far below within-model performance and barely\n    above the majority-class baseline. Cross-family transfer (Llama↔Qwen) is at chance.\n    Worst-tier reviewers use this as evidence against the paper: \"Safety encoding\n    is model-specific. This means every new model needs its own probe training with\n    labeled commitment points — a supervision cost the paper downplays. If safety\n    commitment were a universal phenomenon, SOME geometric structure should transfer.\n    The complete failure suggests the probes are learning model-specific artifacts,\n    not a generalizable safety signal.\" This finding is consistent with the selectivity\n    control task (7.5-18.5pp) but reviewers argue selectivity merely shows the probes\n    aren''t random — not that they detect genuine safety commitment vs idiosyncratic\n    model behavior.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\n    columns:\n    - Train →\n    - R1-8B\n    - OT-7B\n    - R1-32B\n    - QwQ-32B\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - 0.817 (self)\n      - —\n      - '0.522'\n      - '0.595'\n    - - OT-7B\n      - —\n      - 0.688 (self)\n      - —\n      - —\n    - - R1-32B\n      - '0.583'\n      - —\n      - 0.826 (self)\n      - '0.673'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - —\n      - —\n      - '0.673'\n      - 0.740 (self)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Heatmap: transfer accuracy matrix. Diagonal (self) shows 69-83%. Off-diagonal\n    all below 68%. Cross-family cells (Llama↔Qwen) near chance (50-60%).'\n  analysis: 'Complete transfer failure. Worst-tier reviewers conclude: \"The paper\n    claims to study ''safety commitment detection'' as a general phenomenon, but the\n    probes are entirely model-specific. This means the method has zero practical scalability\n    — you need labeled data for every model you want to monitor. Combined with the\n    engineering cost of white-box access, the deployment story collapses.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Lead time analysis across models\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Evaluate HS probe lead time distributions on all 4 models. Compare\n    HS vs text lead times. Lead time = steps between first detection and commitment\n    point.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_hs_lead_mean: -0.1\n    r1_8b_hs_lead_note: HS detects AT commitment, not before\n    r1_8b_text_lead_mean: 10.3\n    r1_8b_text_lead_inflated: high text lead is artifact of high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy)\n    ot_7b_hs_lead_mean: 21.6\n    ot_7b_text_lead_mean: 45.5\n    ot_7b_lead_note: absolute values inflated by long traces (~80 sentences)\n    qwq_32b_hs_lead_mean_range: -1.2 to +1.9\n    qwq_32b_text_lead_mean_range: 3.7 to 12.3\n    qwq_32b_hs_lead_negative: true\n    qwq_32b_position_baseline_lead: better than HS\n    r1_32b_hs_lead_mean: +11.7 to +13.3\n    r1_32b_hs_lead_positive: true\n    r1_32b_coverage_at_significant_w: 0.25\n    r1_32b_lead_caveat: only 5/20 traces detected at W=1\n    gru_r1_8b_lead: +4.5 ± 3.0\n    gru_r1_8b_worst_seed: '+0.9'\n  result_rationale: 'Lead time results are a mixed bag that worst-tier reviewers weaponize:\n    (1) R1-8B HS lead is effectively zero — HS detects AT commitment, not before.\n    Only GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9). Text lead of +10.3\n    is misleadingly high (artifact of trigger-happy detection with 20.5% pre-CP FPR).\n    (2) QwQ-32B: HS lead time is NEGATIVE (-1.2 to +1.9 steps) while text detects\n    3.7-12.3 steps earlier. Position baseline (bal_acc=0.746) beats HS (bal_acc=0.740).\n    A reviewer writes: \"for QwQ, just tracking relative position in the trace outperforms\n    hidden-state monitoring — why bother with white-box access?\" (3) R1-32B has excellent\n    HS lead (+13 steps) but only 25% coverage — meaning 15/20 traces are undetected.\n    Reviewer: \"a detector that works on 5 traces is an anecdote, not a system.\" (4)\n    OT-7B lead times are inflated by long traces. Overall: 2/4 models have HS lead\n    ≤ 0, 1/4 has only 25% coverage, and 1/4 has inflated absolute values. No model\n    presents a clean HS lead time story.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Lead Time Comparison: HS vs Text vs Position'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - HS Lead (steps)\n    - Text Lead (steps)\n    - Position BA\n    - HS BA\n    - HS > Position?\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B (W=15)\n      - −0.1\n      - +10.3†\n      - —\n      - '0.802'\n      - —\n    - - OT-7B (W=3)\n      - +21.6‡\n      - +45.5‡\n      - —\n      - '0.688'\n      - —\n    - - QwQ-32B (W=3)\n      - −1.2 to +1.9\n      - +3.7 to +12.3\n      - '0.746'\n      - '0.740'\n      - 'No'\n    - - R1-32B (W=1)\n      - +11.7 to +13.3\n      - —\n      - '0.686'\n      - '0.826'\n      - Yes (25% cov)\n  figures_description:\n  - Per-model lead time distribution boxplot. QwQ and R1-8B HS centered near zero.\n    R1-32B shows positive lead but with 75% of traces undetected (marked as missing).\n    Text leads generally positive but with high FPR caveats.\n  analysis: 'Worst-tier reviewers identify lead time as the practical dealbreaker:\n    \"The paper claims HS monitoring enables early detection, but 2/4 models show HS\n    detecting LATER than text. The one model with good lead time (R1-32B) detects\n    only 25% of traces. A deployment team would choose text monitoring over HS monitoring\n    for lead time alone, saving the engineering cost of hidden-state extraction.\"\n\n    '\n- name: TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (all 4 models)\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: 'Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace covariance across all 4 models.\n    Tests whether geometric distance-based methods (TrajGuard-style) can detect safety\n    commitment.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_separation: 0.07\n    ot_7b_separation: 0.03\n    r1_32b_separation: 0.15\n    qwq_32b_separation: 0.2\n    max_separation_any_model: 0.2\n    all_models_fail: true\n    false_alarm_exceeds_tp_all_thresholds: true\n    signal_location: minor PCA components, not principal directions\n    r1_8b_safe_md: 6.66\n    r1_8b_post_md: 6.73\n    r1_32b_safe_md: 6.73\n    r1_32b_post_md: 6.88\n    qwq_32b_safe_md: 3.22\n    qwq_32b_post_md: 3.42\n  result_rationale: 'Mahalanobis distance fails on all 4 models (max separation 0.20\n    on QwQ, FA rate ≥ TP rate at every threshold). The signal lives in minor PCA components,\n    not principal variance directions — Mahalanobis, which weights by inverse covariance,\n    is structurally blind to this geometry. Worst-tier reviewers have mixed reactions:\n    (1) \"Mahalanobis failing is expected — Bolukbasi et al. 2016 and subsequent work\n    showed safety-relevant features are often orthogonal to principal variance. This\n    negative result is predictable, not informative.\" (2) \"You tested one geometric\n    baseline. What about k-NN distance, local outlier factor, or isolation forests?\n    The negative result space is underexplored.\" The finding supports the \"existing\n    methods fail\" claim but reviewers rate it as incremental — failure of one known-limited\n    baseline is not a contribution.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Mahalanobis Distance Separation (4 models)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Layer\n    - Safe MD\n    - Post-CP MD\n    - Separation\n    - FA ≥ TP?\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - L14\n      - '6.66'\n      - '6.73'\n      - '+0.07'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - OT-7B\n      - L16\n      - '7.68'\n      - '7.71'\n      - '+0.03'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - R1-32B\n      - L63\n      - '6.73'\n      - '6.88'\n      - '+0.15'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - L60\n      - '3.22'\n      - '3.42'\n      - '+0.20'\n      - 'Yes'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'MD time-series: safe (blue) and jailbreak (red) traces completely overlapping\n    on all 4 models. No visible discontinuity at commitment point.'\n  analysis: 'Universal Mahalanobis failure confirms safety signal is geometrically\n    inaccessible to distance-based methods. But worst-tier reviewers dismiss this:\n    \"Showing Mahalanobis doesn''t work is a paragraph in a methods section, not a\n    contribution. The field already knows unsupervised geometric methods struggle\n    with fine-grained behavioral distinctions.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Bootstrap confidence intervals + threshold-independent metrics\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: '10000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across 4 models. Holm-Bonferroni\n    correction. AUROC, precision-recall curves, detection delay CDF.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    holm_bonferroni_pass: 12/12\n    r1_8b_precision_delta: +22.4pp\n    r1_8b_precision_p: <0.001\n    ot_7b_precision_delta: +21.9pp\n    ot_7b_precision_p: <0.001\n    qwq_32b_precision_delta: +23.6pp\n    qwq_32b_precision_p: <0.001\n    qwq_32b_fpr_p: 0.004\n    r1_32b_precision_delta: +15.4pp\n    r1_32b_precision_p: <0.001\n    r1_32b_fpr_p: 0.007\n    r1_32b_fpr_marginal_note: p=0.007 passes HB threshold 0.025 but barely\n    marginal_tests_count: 2\n    all_precision_deltas_positive: true\n    precision_range: +15.4 to +23.6pp\n    r1_32b_ci_width_ba: ~0.15\n    qwq_32b_ci_width_ba: ~0.12\n    r1_8b_ci_width_ba: ~0.06\n  result_rationale: 'All 12/12 tests pass Holm-Bonferroni, which is statistically\n    clean. But worst-tier reviewers attack the PRACTICAL significance, not the statistical\n    significance: (1) \"Of course 12/12 pass — you selected the best layer AND the\n    best window per model, then tested. The researcher degrees of freedom (4 models\n    × 64 layers × 7 windows × 5 metrics) dwarf the 12 tests corrected for.\" (2) \"The\n    CIs for 32B models are extremely wide (±0.15 for R1-32B bal_acc). Statistical\n    significance with n=20 means the effect is large, but the PRECISION of the estimate\n    is poor. You know HS is better somewhere between +3pp and +34pp — that''s not\n    actionable.\" (3) \"R1-32B FPR p=0.007 and QwQ FPR p=0.004 are the most marginal\n    passing tests. A 10001st bootstrap replicate could flip either.\" (4) \"Holm-Bonferroni\n    corrects for 12 tests, but the true multiplicity (including layer/window selection)\n    is orders of magnitude larger.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12, α=0.05)\n    columns:\n    - Rank\n    - Model\n    - Metric\n    - p-value\n    - HB Threshold\n    - Pass\n    rows:\n    - - 1\n      - R1-8B\n      - precision\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0042'\n      - ✅\n    - - 2\n      - OT-7B\n      - bal_acc\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0045'\n      - ✅\n    - - 3\n      - OT-7B\n      - precision\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0050'\n      - ✅\n    - - 4\n      - OT-7B\n      - FPR\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0056'\n      - ✅\n    - - 5\n      - QwQ\n      - bal_acc\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0063'\n      - ✅\n    - - 6\n      - QwQ\n      - precision\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0071'\n      - ✅\n    - - 7\n      - R1-32B\n      - bal_acc\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0083'\n      - ✅\n    - - 8\n      - R1-32B\n      - precision\n      - <0.001\n      - '0.0100'\n      - ✅\n    - - 9\n      - QwQ\n      - FPR\n      - '0.004'\n      - '0.0125'\n      - ✅\n    - - 10\n      - R1-8B\n      - bal_acc\n      - '0.005'\n      - '0.0167'\n      - ✅\n    - - 11\n      - R1-32B\n      - FPR\n      - '0.007'\n      - '0.0250'\n      - ✅\n    - - 12\n      - R1-8B\n      - FPR\n      - '0.044'\n      - '0.0500'\n      - ✅\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot: HS-text precision delta with 95% CI per model. All positive. R1-32B\n    CI is widest (+3pp to +28pp), reflecting n=20 limitation.'\n  - 'Bootstrap distribution of HS-text AUROC difference: 7-8B models clearly positive,\n    32B models positive but with wider spread.'\n  analysis: 'Statistical significance is established but reviewers find it insufficient:\n    \"The paper''s statistical methodology is sound, but the story it tells is ''HS\n    is significantly better by 15-24pp precision at best-case layer/window selection.''\n    For an EMNLP Main paper, we need either (a) a novel method that achieves these\n    gains, or (b) a surprising finding. Neither: linear probes on hidden states are\n    standard, and it''s unsurprising they outperform text embeddings (hidden states\n    are an information superset of text).\"\n\n    '\n- name: Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all models)\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Systematic window size sweep on all 4 models. Characterize precision-coverage\n    tradeoff and identify optimal W per model.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    coverage_decreases_with_w_all_models: true\n    precision_increases_with_w_all_models: true\n    fpr_decreases_with_w_all_models: true\n    r1_8b_best_bal_acc_w: 25\n    r1_8b_w25_precision: 0.822\n    r1_8b_w25_coverage: 0.488\n    r1_8b_w25_fpr_step: 0.048\n    r1_32b_best_bal_acc_w: 10\n    r1_32b_w10_precision: 0.609\n    r1_32b_w10_coverage: 0.7\n    r1_32b_w10_fpr_step: 0.234\n    qwq_32b_best_bal_acc_w: 25\n    qwq_32b_w25_precision: 0.618\n    qwq_32b_w25_coverage: 0.579\n    qwq_32b_w25_fpr_step: 0.169\n    ot_7b_hs_peaks_w3: true\n    ot_7b_w3_bal_acc: 0.688\n    ot_7b_w25_bal_acc: 0.659\n    tradeoff_universal: larger W → higher precision, lower coverage, lower FPR\n  result_rationale: 'Window ablation reveals a clean precision-sensitivity tradeoff\n    that holds across all models. Worst-tier reviewers dismiss this: (1) \"The coverage-decreasing-with-W\n    finding is trivially expected — requiring K=5 consecutive crossings with a larger\n    window means fewer traces reach the threshold. This is a property of the detection\n    criterion K, not an insight about temporal probing.\" (2) \"The optimal W varies\n    by model (W=3 for OT-7B, W=10 for R1-32B, W=25 for QwQ and R1-8B). This means\n    W is another model-specific hyperparameter to tune — combined with layer selection,\n    you need per-model layer AND window tuning. The ''systematic evaluation'' framing\n    hides the fact that this is just exhaustive grid search.\" (3) \"At the best W per\n    model, coverage ranges from 49% (R1-8B) to 70% (R1-32B). Missing 30-50% of safety\n    commitment events is not deployment-ready.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Precision-Coverage Tradeoff Summary (all 4 models)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - W=1 Prec/Cov\n    - W=3 Prec/Cov\n    - W=15 Prec/Cov\n    - W=25 Prec/Cov\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - 0.693/0.721\n      - 0.723/0.698\n      - 0.777/0.512\n      - 0.822/0.488\n    - - OT-7B\n      - —/—\n      - 0.466/0.532\n      - —/—\n      - —/—\n    - - R1-32B\n      - 0.549/0.800\n      - 0.560/0.800\n      - 0.591/0.700\n      - 0.566/0.700\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - 0.544/0.789\n      - 0.565/0.737\n      - 0.582/0.684\n      - 0.618/0.579\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: precision (solid) and coverage (dashed) vs W for each model. Clear\n    inverse relationship. Shaded region shows ''deployment-acceptable'' zone (precision>0.7,\n    coverage>0.6) — no model reaches it for any W.'\n  analysis: 'The ablation confirms the tradeoff is universal but no operating point\n    achieves both high precision AND high coverage. Worst-tier reviewer: \"Table 3\n    is the paper''s most honest result. At no window size does any model achieve >0.7\n    precision AND >0.6 coverage simultaneously. This is not a tuning problem — it''s\n    a fundamental performance ceiling.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Layer selection ablation (5 metrics × 4 models)\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'Evaluate all layers with temporal probes on 32B models under 5 different\n    selection metrics. Demonstrate crossing_rate bias and metric-dependent layer selection.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    crossing_rate_selects_shallow_32b: true\n    pure_bal_acc_selects_middle_32b: true\n    threshold_dependent_select_deep_32b: true\n    qwq_l2_vs_l60_precision_gain: +11.5pp\n    r1_32b_l10_vs_l63_precision_gain: +4.1pp\n    loo_cv_stability: 65-68% dominant fold\n    r1_8b_consistent: true\n    ot_7b_varied: true\n    bal_acc_layer_vs_precision_layer_diverge_32b: true\n    qwq_l30_bal_acc: 0.751\n    qwq_l60_bal_acc: 0.71\n    r1_32b_l47_bal_acc: 0.829\n    r1_32b_l63_bal_acc: 0.759\n    ba_optimal_different_from_precision_optimal: true\n  result_rationale: 'The layer selection ablation reveals a genuine pattern (biased\n    metric → shallow layers → masked HS advantage), but worst-tier reviewers split\n    on its significance: (1) The supportive reading: \"This is a useful observation\n    — researchers using bal_acc × crossing_rate will get misleading results.\" (2)\n    The dismissive reading (dominant in worst tier): \"This is expected behavior of\n    a poorly chosen metric. The composite bal_acc × crossing_rate was never standard\n    — it''s the authors'' own construction (or a rare one). Showing it''s bad doesn''t\n    advance the field because nobody was using it.\" (3) The circularity concern: \"You\n    select layers by precision/FPR, then evaluate by precision/FPR. The D044 BA-layer\n    robustness check partially addresses this (16/16 HB pass on BA-selected layers),\n    but the reviewer asks: ''if BA-selected layers also show HS advantage, why not\n    just use BA selection? What''s the contribution of the 5-metric comparison?''\"\n    (4) \"LOO-CV stability of 65-68% means 32-35% of folds select a DIFFERENT layer.\n    This is not the ''robust selection'' the paper claims.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Metric × Model → Selected Layer (with HS-Text precision delta)\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - R1-8B Layer (ΔPrec)\n    - OT-7B Layer (ΔPrec)\n    - QwQ Layer (ΔPrec)\n    - R1-32B Layer (ΔPrec)\n    rows:\n    - - bal_acc × cr\n      - L0 (+var)\n      - L0 (+var)\n      - L2 (+5.8pp NS)\n      - L10 (+9.4pp NS)\n    - - pure bal_acc\n      - L12 (+17.8pp)\n      - L9 (+25.4pp)\n      - L30 (+18.0pp)\n      - L47 (+13.7pp)\n    - - precision\n      - L16 (+22.4pp)\n      - L16 (+21.9pp)\n      - L60 (+23.6pp)\n      - L63 (+15.4pp)\n    - - pre_cp_fpr\n      - L16 (−7.4pp)\n      - L16 (−17.1pp)\n      - L60 (−9.8pp)\n      - L63 (−14.6pp)\n    - - composite\n      - L16 (+22.4pp)\n      - L12 (+var)\n      - L60 (+23.6pp)\n      - L63 (+15.4pp)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Alluvial diagram: metric (left) → selected layer depth band (right), colored\n    by model. Crossing_rate-contaminated metric routes to shallow band; all others\n    route to mid/deep.'\n  analysis: 'The ablation is thorough but worst-tier reviewers question whether it\n    constitutes a contribution: \"Sensitivity analysis is a sanity check, not a finding.\n    The paper would be better if it had used the right metric from the start and reported\n    results at correct layers — making the sensitivity analysis unnecessary for the\n    paper''s core claims.\"\n\n    '\n- name: 16-label taxonomy supervision\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'Train probes with fine-grained safety labels (Type 1/2/3/4 × pre/post)\n    instead of binary commitment. Evaluate whether richer supervision improves detection.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_16label_acc: 0.41\n    r1_8b_binary_collapse_acc: 0.825\n    r1_8b_binary_collapse_delta: +0.8pp\n    ot_7b_16label_acc: 0.35\n    ot_7b_binary_collapse_delta: +1.1pp\n    r1_32b_16label_acc: 0.28\n    r1_32b_binary_collapse_delta: +0.6pp\n    qwq_32b_16label_acc: 0.26\n    qwq_32b_binary_collapse_delta: +0.9pp\n    fine_grained_improvement_marginal: true\n    max_collapse_delta: +1.1pp\n  result_rationale: 'Fine-grained supervision provides marginal benefit (+0.6-1.1pp\n    after binary collapse) across all models. The 16-class accuracy is low (26-41%)\n    because the classes are imbalanced and transition types are not cleanly separable\n    in hidden-state space. Worst-tier reviewers react: (1) \"Marginal improvement from\n    richer supervision confirms the probe is already extracting most of the available\n    linear signal in binary mode. This supports the reviewer''s argument that linear\n    probes are saturated — not that the methodology is optimal, but that the information\n    ceiling is low.\" (2) \"The 16-label experiment was motivated by reviewer concern\n    about ''underusing available supervision,'' but the result shows supervision quality\n    doesn''t matter much. This undermines the paper''s implicit argument that careful\n    methodology (layer selection, temporal context) is what matters — it seems the\n    representation quality is the binding constraint.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 16-Label vs Binary Supervision\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Binary Acc\n    - 16-Label Acc\n    - 16→Binary Collapse\n    - Δ vs Binary\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - '0.817'\n      - '0.41'\n      - '0.825'\n      - +0.8pp\n    - - OT-7B\n      - '0.688'\n      - '0.35'\n      - '0.699'\n      - +1.1pp\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - '0.740'\n      - '0.26'\n      - '0.749'\n      - +0.9pp\n    - - R1-32B\n      - '0.826'\n      - '0.28'\n      - '0.832'\n      - +0.6pp\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Confusion matrix (16-class) on R1-8B: heavy diagonal on safe categories, near-uniform\n    on unsafe subcategories — probes cannot distinguish commitment subtypes.'\n  analysis: 'Fine-grained supervision dead-end. Worst-tier implication: \"The information\n    ceiling for linear probes on single-layer hidden states is fundamentally limited.\n    Trying harder with better labels or more metrics can''t overcome this.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Oracle decomposition\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: 'Analyze what the oracle label sequence encodes that hidden-state probes\n    cannot access. Compare oracle lead (+31 steps on R1-8B) to probe lead (~0 steps).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_oracle_lead: '+31.0'\n    r1_8b_best_probe_lead: +4.5 (GRU)\n    r1_8b_oracle_gap: '26.5'\n    r1_8b_label_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.82\n    r1_8b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.95\n    label_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b: 1.38\n    hs_entropy_pre_cp_r1_8b: 0.41\n    r1_32b_oracle_lead: '+28'\n    r1_32b_best_probe_lead: +13 (W=1, 25% cov)\n    qwq_oracle_lead: '+27'\n    qwq_best_probe_lead: −1.2 to +1.9\n    pattern_finding: Labels show gradual safety erosion pre-CP; hidden states show\n      step-function at CP\n  result_rationale: 'Oracle decomposition reveals a 26-28 step gap between what''s\n    detectable in labels vs hidden states. The label sequence shows gradual pre-CP\n    patterns (increasing unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe streaks) that hidden\n    states don''t track. Worst-tier reviewers draw a harsh conclusion: \"The 31-step\n    oracle lead proves that safety commitment is a GRADUAL process visible in the\n    model''s outputs. But the hidden-state probe can only detect it at or after the\n    commitment point (lead ≈ 0). This means hidden states encode the CURRENT safety\n    state but not the TRAJECTORY toward commitment. For deployment, trajectory prediction\n    is what matters — catching the commitment after it happens is too late. The oracle\n    gap is not a puzzle for Discussion; it''s evidence that the entire hidden-state\n    monitoring approach is fundamentally limited. Text-based monitoring that tracks\n    reasoning trajectory (even with higher FPR) provides earlier warning.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Oracle vs Best Probe Lead Time\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Oracle Lead\n    - Best Probe Lead\n    - Method\n    - Gap\n    - Hidden State Entropy (pre-CP)\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - '+31.0'\n      - '+4.5'\n      - GRU multi-layer\n      - '26.5'\n      - '0.41'\n    - - OT-7B\n      - ~+30\n      - +33.6 (GRU)\n      - GRU multi-layer\n      - ~0†\n      - —\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - '+27'\n      - −1.2 to +1.9\n      - temporal LR\n      - 25-28\n      - —\n    - - R1-32B\n      - '+28'\n      - '+13.3'\n      - temporal LR W=1\n      - 15 (25% cov)\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Pre-CP hidden state trajectory (Fisher directions): R1-8B shows minimal drift\n    before CP, then sharp transition. Labels show gradual escalation starting 20-30\n    steps before CP.'\n  analysis: 'The oracle decomposition is the worst-tier''s strongest negative finding:\n    hidden states are fundamentally reactive (encoding current state) rather than\n    predictive (encoding trajectory). Reviewer: \"This paper demonstrates that hidden-state\n    monitoring is a lagging indicator. For safety deployment, you need leading indicators.\n    Text-based trajectory analysis, while noisier, is the better research direction.\"\n\n    '\n- name: OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL-trained)\n  plan_id: exp-011\n  description: '4th model completing the 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Full pipeline:\n    layer sweep, temporal probes, HS vs text comparison, GRU, selectivity control\n    task.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    best_layer: 16\n    best_layer_depth_pct: 57.1\n    best_hs_bal_acc_w3: 0.688\n    best_hs_precision_w3: 0.466\n    best_hs_pre_cp_fpr: 0.1\n    bootstrap_3_of_3_hb: true\n    bootstrap_bal_acc_delta: +10.7pp\n    bootstrap_precision_delta: +21.9pp\n    bootstrap_fpr_delta: −17.1pp\n    selectivity_pp: 18.3\n    n_traces: 228\n    gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.746\n    gru_multi_lead: '+33.6'\n    gru_multi_fpr: 0.274\n    hs_temporal_saturation_w3: true\n    text_temporal_w25_best: 0.614\n  result_rationale: 'OT-7B is the cleanest single-model evidence for HS advantage\n    (3/3 HB, highest selectivity 18.3pp, largest n=228 traces). But worst-tier reviewers\n    are unimpressed by adding a 4th model that confirms the known pattern: (1) \"A\n    4th model at 7B showing the same result doesn''t address the 32B caveats. The\n    paper already has two 7-8B models that work. The question is whether HS monitoring\n    scales, and OT-7B can''t answer that.\" (2) \"OT-7B has its own caveats: HS temporal\n    saturation at W=3 (bal_acc declines at larger W), and absolute performance is\n    still below R1-8B (best bal_acc 68.8% vs 80.2%). The ''cleanest evidence'' is\n    still only 69% balanced accuracy — barely better than a biased coin.\" (3) \"GRU\n    lead time of +33.6 steps sounds impressive until you note OT-7B traces average\n    ~80 sentences — normalize by trace length and the lead time fraction is similar\n    to other models.\" (4) \"FPR of 27.4% for GRU means ~1 in 4 safe steps triggers\n    the detector. At 80-sentence traces, that''s ~20 false alarms per safe trace.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: OT-7B Full Pipeline Summary\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - bal_acc\n    - precision\n    - pre-CP FPR\n    - coverage\n    - lead\n    rows:\n    - - HS temporal W=3 L16\n      - '0.688'\n      - '0.466'\n      - '0.100'\n      - '0.532'\n      - '+21.6'\n    - - Text temporal W=25\n      - '0.614'\n      - '0.329'\n      - '0.161'\n      - '0.660'\n      - '+45.5'\n    - - GRU multi L13-19\n      - '0.746'\n      - —\n      - '0.274'\n      - '0.645'\n      - '+33.6'\n    - - HS-Text Δ (HB)\n      - +10.7pp ✅\n      - +21.9pp ✅\n      - −17.1pp ✅\n      - —\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - '2×2 architecture×scale matrix: Fisher discriminability + HS-text precision delta.\n    Both 7-8B cells show large delta; both 32B cells show delta with model-specific\n    caveats.'\n  analysis: 'OT-7B strengthens the 7-8B story but worst-tier reviewers note: \"The\n    2×2 matrix now has two strong cells (7-8B) and two weak cells (32B). The paper''s\n    breadth claim (''4 models'') is technically true but misleading — 2/4 models have\n    serious practical caveats that make the HS advantage non-deployable.\"\n\n    '\n- name: GRU/Attention probe upper bound (32B models)\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: 'GRU sequence model on 32B models at corrected layers. Tests whether\n    nonlinear sequence modeling can recover signal that linear temporal probes miss.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_gru_single_bal_acc: 0.675\n    r1_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.655\n    r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: +8.8 to +9.1\n    r1_32b_gru_fpr: 0.114 to 0.135\n    r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.23 to 0.30\n    r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: −6pp bal_acc (GRU worse)\n    qwq_32b_gru_single_bal_acc: 0.694\n    qwq_32b_gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.724\n    qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: '+15.9'\n    qwq_32b_gru_fpr: 0.289\n    qwq_32b_gru_coverage: 0.84\n    qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: −3.7pp bal_acc (GRU worse at bal_acc, better at coverage)\n    ot_7b_gru_multi_bal_acc: 0.746\n    ot_7b_gru_multi_lead: '+33.6'\n    ot_7b_gru_fpr: 0.274\n    gru_worse_than_lr_3_of_4: true\n    gru_note: GRU trades accuracy for coverage/lead — higher FPR drives earlier detection\n  result_rationale: 'GRU does NOT outperform temporal LR on balanced accuracy for\n    3/4 models. On R1-32B (n=20 test), GRU severely overfits — bal_acc drops 6pp below\n    LR. On QwQ, GRU achieves higher coverage and lead time but at 2× the FPR. This\n    confirms linear probes are sufficient (or even superior given small samples).\n    Worst-tier reviewers use this to attack novelty: \"The paper''s method is logistic\n    regression on hidden states with a sliding window. That''s not a contribution\n    — it''s the simplest possible baseline. The fact that GRU and MLP don''t help\n    confirms the method space is trivial, which means the paper''s ''systematic comparison\n    of 7 methods'' is comparing variations of the obvious approach (linear probe)\n    against methods that were never designed for this task (Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CCS).\n    The negative results are predictable, not informative.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: GRU vs Temporal LR (32B models, corrected layers)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - LR bal_acc\n    - GRU bal_acc\n    - Δ\n    - GRU FPR\n    - LR FPR\n    rows:\n    - - R1-32B (L63 W=10)\n      - '0.737'\n      - ~0.675\n      - −6.2pp\n      - '0.135'\n      - '0.234'\n    - - QwQ-32B (L60 W=5)\n      - '0.761'\n      - '0.724'\n      - −3.7pp\n      - '0.289'\n      - '0.217'\n    - - OT-7B (L16 multi)\n      - '0.688'\n      - '0.746'\n      - +5.8pp\n      - '0.274'\n      - '0.100'\n    - - R1-8B (L14 multi)\n      - '0.806'\n      - '0.809'\n      - +0.3pp\n      - '0.165'\n      - '0.097'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter: method complexity (x: LR/GRU/MLP) vs bal_acc (y), per model. No consistent\n    trend — complexity doesn''t help. Error bars from multi-seed show high variance\n    for GRU.'\n  analysis: 'GRU fails to improve over LR on most models, confirming linear probes\n    are at the information ceiling. Worst-tier reviewer: \"The paper''s headline is\n    ''HS beats text.'' The method is logistic regression. The contribution is... careful\n    hyperparameter selection? This is a well-executed experiment report, not a methods\n    paper.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal LR)\n  plan_id: exp-013\n  description: 'Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings + temporal window LR on\n    CoT text of all 4 models. Critical comparison establishing what text monitoring\n    achieves without hidden-state access.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_text_static_acc: 0.661\n    r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_acc: 0.678\n    r1_8b_text_temporal_w15_auroc: 0.759\n    r1_8b_hs_acc_advantage: +15-18pp\n    ot_7b_text_best_bal_acc: 0.614\n    ot_7b_text_best_precision: 0.329\n    ot_7b_text_best_fpr: 0.161\n    qwq_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc: 0.65\n    qwq_32b_text_precision: 0.421\n    r1_32b_text_temporal_bal_acc: 0.646\n    r1_32b_text_precision: 0.45\n    hs_vs_text_precision_gap_range: +15.4 to +23.6pp\n    text_requires_zero_infra_change: true\n    text_precision_all_models: 0.329-0.487\n    hs_precision_all_models: 0.466-0.776\n  result_rationale: 'Text baseline performs consistently across all models (bal_acc\n    0.61-0.68, precision 0.33-0.49). HS probes outperform by +15-24pp precision, which\n    is statistically significant (12/12 HB). But worst-tier reviewers attack the COST-BENEFIT\n    ratio, not the statistical claim: (1) \"Text probes need zero infrastructure change\n    — you monitor the CoT output that''s already available. HS probes require white-box\n    access to model internals, which means: (a) running the model locally (can''t\n    use API), (b) modifying the inference pipeline to extract intermediate activations,\n    (c) per-model layer selection + probe training. The 15-24pp precision gain comes\n    at enormous engineering cost.\" (2) \"At 33-49% text precision, you get 1 true alarm\n    per 2-3 triggers. At 47-78% HS precision, you get 1 per 1.3-2.1 triggers. Is that\n    difference worth instrumenting your entire inference stack? For most deployment\n    teams, no.\" (3) \"Text probes improve with better embeddings (BGE-large, domain-fine-tuned).\n    The paper tested only all-MiniLM-L6-v2 — the weakest common baseline. A reviewer\n    notes: ''Use GPT-4 as the text classifier and the gap likely narrows to <5pp.''\"\n    (4) \"The BGE-large comparison (exp_bge_large_text_comparison) showed only 1/4\n    HB pass for HS vs BGE-large — the gap narrows significantly with a better text\n    encoder.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: HS vs Text Precision Gap (+ engineering cost annotation)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - HS Precision\n    - Text Precision\n    - Δ\n    - HS Infra Cost\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - 0.777 (W=15)\n      - 0.487 (W=15)\n      - +29.0pp\n      - white-box, L14\n    - - OT-7B\n      - 0.466 (W=3)\n      - 0.329 (W=25)\n      - +13.7pp\n      - white-box, L16\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - 0.578 (W=3)\n      - 0.421 (W=best)\n      - +15.7pp\n      - white-box, L60\n    - - R1-32B\n      - 0.603 (W=1)\n      - 0.450 (W=best)\n      - +15.3pp\n      - white-box, L63\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cost-benefit scatter: x-axis = engineering complexity (text=1, HS=3), y-axis\n    = precision. Text cluster at (1, 0.33-0.49), HS cluster at (3, 0.47-0.78). Marginal\n    ROI line shows diminishing returns.'\n  analysis: 'The text baseline is the worst-tier reviewer''s strongest weapon: \"A\n    15-24pp precision gain sounds meaningful until you consider: (a) text monitoring\n    costs $0 in infrastructure, (b) HS monitoring requires white-box access + per-model\n    tuning, (c) the BGE-large result shows the gap shrinks with better text encoders,\n    (d) safe FPR is 39-62% regardless of modality — both approaches produce unusable\n    false alarm rates. The paper should honestly state: ''HS probes are measurably\n    but marginally better than text probes. Whether the margin justifies white-box\n    access depends on deployment constraints. For most practical settings, text monitoring\n    suffices.''\"\n\n    '\n- name: BOCPD/CUSUM on all 4 models\n  plan_id: exp-014\n  description: 'Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection + CUSUM on probe score sequences.\n    Tests whether advanced change-point methods can achieve positive lead time.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_bocpd_detection: 0.93\n    r1_8b_bocpd_lead: '+3.45'\n    r1_8b_cusum_lead: '+0.89'\n    r1_8b_fcp_baseline_lead: '+4.89'\n    r1_8b_fcp_beats_bocpd_on_lead: true\n    ot_7b_bocpd_detection: 0.0\n    ot_7b_bocpd_note: W=3 probe scores too spiky, Gaussian model fails\n    ot_7b_fcp_best_detection: 0.894\n    ot_7b_fcp_best_lead: '+11.83'\n    r1_32b_bocpd_detection: 0.0\n    qwq_32b_bocpd_detection: 0.0\n    r1_32b_cusum_detection: 0.85\n    r1_32b_cusum_lead: '+2.59'\n    qwq_32b_cusum_detection: 0.737\n    qwq_32b_cusum_lead: '+9.86'\n    bocpd_works_only_r1_8b: true\n    cusum_universally_worse_than_fcp: true\n  result_rationale: 'BOCPD fails on 3/4 models (0% detection on OT-7B, R1-32B, QwQ).\n    Only R1-8B works (93%) because R1-8B has gradual probe score transitions that\n    match BOCPD''s Gaussian change-point model. Other models have abrupt transitions\n    (OT-7B) or weak signals (32B) that BOCPD''s parametric assumptions can''t handle.\n    CUSUM detects some traces but with worse lead than simple first-crossing-point\n    (FCP) threshold. Worst-tier reviewers: (1) \"BOCPD and CUSUM were never designed\n    for this signal type. Testing them is due diligence, but their failure is predictable\n    — these methods assume stationary-then-shifted Gaussian data, while probe scores\n    are bounded [0,1] and often spiky.\" (2) \"The negative result strengthens the ''nothing\n    works well'' narrative, but the method selection is questionable. Why not test\n    HMM, Viterbi decoding, or learned change-point detectors? The paper tests 2 off-the-shelf\n    methods and declares change-point detection a dead end.\" (3) \"Simple FCP threshold\n    beats BOCPD on mean lead time for R1-8B. The ''advanced'' methods add nothing.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Change-Point Detection Cross-Model Summary\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - BOCPD Det%\n    - BOCPD Lead\n    - CUSUM Det%\n    - CUSUM Lead\n    - FCP Det%\n    - FCP Lead\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - 93%\n      - '+3.45'\n      - 65%\n      - '+0.89'\n      - 65%\n      - '+4.89'\n    - - OT-7B\n      - 0%\n      - —\n      - 75%†\n      - +7.46†\n      - 89%\n      - '+11.83'\n    - - R1-32B\n      - 0%\n      - —\n      - 85%\n      - '+2.59'\n      - 95%\n      - '+5.68'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - 0%\n      - —\n      - 74%\n      - '+9.86'\n      - 79%\n      - '+9.40'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Probe score time-series with change-point annotations: R1-8B shows gradual transition\n    detected by BOCPD. OT-7B shows abrupt step-function that BOCPD misses. 32B models\n    show noisy flat signal.'\n  analysis: 'Complete BOCPD failure on 3/4 models and CUSUM inferiority to simple\n    thresholding. Worst-tier reviewer: \"The paper tests two change-point algorithms\n    that were designed for very different signal types, finds they don''t work, and\n    claims this as a negative result. But the method selection is so narrow that the\n    negative result is uninformative — you tested the wrong tools.\"\n\n    '\n- name: False positive analysis (safe trace FPR, all methods)\n  plan_id: exp-015\n  description: 'Run all probes on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) across all 4 models.\n    Report safe FPR, pre-CP FPR, precision, and precision-recall curves.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.39\n    r1_8b_text_safe_fpr: 0.385\n    ot_7b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.111-0.175\n    ot_7b_text_safe_fpr: 0.302-0.460\n    qwq_32b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.45-0.50\n    qwq_32b_text_safe_fpr: 0.325-0.425\n    r1_32b_hs_safe_fpr: 0.239\n    r1_32b_text_safe_fpr: 0.31\n    safe_fpr_range_all: 0.111 to 0.521\n    safe_fpr_median: ~0.39\n    gru_safe_fpr: 0.521\n    gru_highest_fpr_note: GRU trades precision for coverage → highest FPR\n    precision_all_methods_range: 0.329 to 0.777\n    no_method_achieves_80pct_precision: true\n    deployment_threshold_precision_70: only R1-8B HS W≥15 exceeds\n  result_rationale: 'Safe FPR ranges from 11% (OT-7B HS static, best case) to 52%\n    (GRU, worst case), with a median around 39%. This is the worst-tier dealbreaker.\n    Reviewer calculation: \"At 39% safe FPR with K=5 consecutive crossings for trace-level\n    detection, a 40-step safe trace has ~39% probability of triggering the detector.\n    In a production system processing 1000 safe traces per day, that''s ~390 false\n    alarms daily. Each requires human review at ~5 minutes → 32.5 person-hours/day\n    of false alarm triage. No deployment team would accept this.\" Even the best case\n    (OT-7B HS 11.1% safe FPR) means 111 false alarms per 1000 safe traces. The worst-tier\n    reviewer''s verdict: \"A safety monitor that fires on 1 in 3 to 1 in 2 safe traces\n    is not a monitor — it''s a random noise generator. The paper should lead with\n    this limitation, not bury it.\" QwQ-32B is particularly problematic: HS safe FPR\n    (45-50%) is HIGHER than text safe FPR (33-43%), meaning HS monitoring generates\n    MORE false alarms on safe traces for QwQ.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Safe Trace FPR Summary (all methods × all models)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - HS Static FPR\n    - HS Temporal FPR\n    - Text Temporal FPR\n    - GRU FPR\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - '0.390'\n      - '0.392'\n      - '0.385'\n      - '0.521'\n    - - OT-7B\n      - '0.111'\n      - '0.175'\n      - '0.460'\n      - —\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - '0.500'\n      - '0.450'\n      - '0.425'\n      - —\n    - - R1-32B\n      - '0.239'\n      - '0.239'\n      - '0.310'\n      - —\n  - name: Deployment Viability Assessment\n    columns:\n    - Criterion\n    - Threshold\n    - Models Meeting Criterion\n    - Best Method\n    rows:\n    - - Safe FPR < 20%\n      - '0.20'\n      - 1/4 (OT-7B HS static)\n      - HS static L16\n    - - Precision > 70%\n      - '0.70'\n      - 1/4 (R1-8B W≥15)\n      - HS temporal W=25\n    - - Coverage > 60%\n      - '0.60'\n      - 2/4 (R1-8B W≤3, R1-32B W≤3)\n      - HS temporal W=1\n    - - All three criteria\n      - —\n      - 0/4\n      - None\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Radar chart per model: axes = safe FPR (inverted), precision, coverage, lead\n    time. No model fills all quadrants. The ''deployment-ready'' region (inner polygon)\n    is unreached.'\n  - 'PR curves: all models. Dashed line at precision=0.7. Only R1-8B HS crosses this\n    line, at recall=0.25.'\n  analysis: 'FPR analysis is the worst-tier''s core evidence for the \"not practically\n    useful\" dismissal. No model meets all three deployment criteria simultaneously.\n    The best model (R1-8B) achieves >70% precision only at W≥15 where coverage drops\n    to 49%. The paper''s deployment framing is untenable. Reframing guidance: \"Position\n    as diagnostic/research tool, explicitly disclaim deployment readiness. Lead with\n    ''none of our 7 methods achieves deployment-ready performance'' as a cautionary\n    finding.\"\n\n    '\n- name: 5-fold CV validation of multi-metric ensemble (7-8B)\n  plan_id: ensemble_cv_7b\n  description: 'Cross-validated ensemble of metric-weighted layer selections on R1-8B\n    and OT-7B. Validates that multi-metric ensemble eliminates layer selection bias\n    under proper CV.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.565\n    r1_8b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.604\n    r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_gap: +19.3pp\n    r1_8b_ensemble_vs_text_p: <0.002\n    ot_7b_ensemble_cv_prec: 0.506\n    ot_7b_best_single_cv_prec: 0.473\n    ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single: true\n    ot_7b_ensemble_beats_best_single_p: 0.033\n    cv_validates_preliminary: true\n    cv_acceptance_probability_delta: +0.01 to +0.03\n  result_rationale: 'CV validation confirms the ensemble result holds (p≤0.002 for\n    ensemble vs text on R1-8B). But worst-tier reviewers are unmoved: (1) \"CV validating\n    an ensemble of layer selection metrics on 7-8B models doesn''t address ANY of\n    our core concerns. The issues are: high safe FPR (39-62%), model-specific caveats\n    at 32B, engineering cost of white-box access, and insufficient novelty. A statistically\n    cleaner layer selection protocol is nice but tangential.\" (2) \"The ensemble precision\n    (0.506-0.565) is actually LOWER than the best single metric on R1-8B (0.604).\n    On OT-7B the ensemble slightly beats the best single metric (0.506 vs 0.473) but\n    the absolute precision is 50% — a coin flip.\" (3) \"This experiment strengthens\n    an already- strong statistical case (12/12 HB already passes). The paper doesn''t\n    need MORE statistical validation; it needs BETTER results or a DIFFERENT framing.\"\n    The CV ensemble moves acceptance probability by +1-3%, insufficient to shift from\n    worst tier.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: Ensemble CV Results (7-8B models)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Ensemble Prec\n    - Best Single Prec\n    - Text Prec\n    - Ens vs Text p\n    rows:\n    - - R1-8B\n      - '0.565'\n      - '0.604'\n      - '0.372'\n      - <0.002\n    - - OT-7B\n      - '0.506'\n      - '0.473'\n      - '0.236'\n      - <0.001\n  figures_description:\n  - 'CV fold boxplot: ensemble precision across 5 folds. Low variance confirms stability\n    but absolute values are mediocre (50-57%).'\n  analysis: 'Ensemble CV is a marginal improvement on an already-validated statistical\n    claim. Worst-tier impact on acceptance: negligible (+1-3%). The bottleneck is\n    not statistical rigor — it''s practical utility and novelty. This experiment is\n    low-cost and worth including in supplementary, but it cannot rescue the paper\n    from worst-tier reviewer concerns.'\n","numLines":1392,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1392}}}
